CinefexSearchData.register("text/22",{"issue":22,"date":"June 1985","pages":[["1","Cover","number 22 $4.75 Canada - $6.50 cinefex"],["2","Closing pages",""],["3","Contents","cinefex Box 20027, Riverside, California 92516. Subscriptions (U.S.): four issues - $15; eight issues issues - $35. Return postage must accompany all unsolicited manuscripts, photos or other materials. Printed by Valley Printers, Sunnymead, California: color separations by ViviColour, City of Industry, California. Contents copyright © 1985 by Don Shay. All rights reserved. Reproduction in whole or in part without written permission is prohibited. contributing editors Adam Eisenberg Paul M. Sammon circulation manager - June 1985. Published quarterly at P.O. ...the journal of cinematic illusions editor & publisher Don Shay associate editor Janine Pourroy Ann Dredla editorial assistant JoDyne Shay 4 Return to Oz It all started eighty-five years ago when L. Frank Baum first captured the hearts and imaginations of children with The Wonderful Wizard of Oz. The book's literary success spawned dozens of stage and screen excursions to the Land of Oz, marked most notably by M-G-M's immortal classic. Repudiating the popular conception of singing and dancing munchkins and vaudevillian backdrops, Walt Disney Productions and director Walter Murch have reexplored Baum's familiar and beloved fantasy world in a dedicated new adaptation - Return to Oz. Producers Gary Kurtz and Paul Maslansky, opticals expert Zoran Perisic, creature designer Lyle Conway and Claymation innovator Will Vinton - with other members of the Oz team - discuss in detail the special brand of wizardry involved in bringing Dorothy Gale's time-honored adventures once again to life. Article by Brad Munson. 42 Bringing Up Baby In the animal-adventure genre there is nothing new under the sun there? For Touchstone Films, the novel twist of casting a most unusual fauna in the title role of Baby was inspiration for the telling of an old tale in a decidedly new way. The Isidoro Raponi-designed infant brontosaurus star was born cinematicaliy in the rain forests of Africa's Ivory Coast following an arduous three-year gestation period. For director B.W.L. Norton and producer Jonathan Taplin, the trials and tribulations of bringing the $14 million production to life involved an exhausting - often harrowing - labor. And the challenge of creating high-tech special effects in a low-tech Third World country furnished all involved with more than their share of real-life thrills and chills. Article by Howard FRONT COVER - The nefarious Nome King from Return to Oz. A brontosaurus and her hatchling swim off into the sunset in Baby- - BACK COVER."],["4","Return to Oz","\"You know how things go in the movie business,\" said Norman Reynolds, remembering when Walter Murch first spoke to him about revisiting the Land of Oz. \"You say: 'Yes, that sounds like a good idea' -and sometimes it happens and sometimes it doesn't. And this happened.\" What happened is Return to Oz, Walt Disney's $25 million return ticket to the Emerald City and environs. With celebrated editor and sound designer Walter Murch at the helm in his first directorial role, and with a team of special effects designers fresh from great successes on films like The Dark Crystal and Greystoke, the opportunity was too good for Reynolds - and many others - to pass up. An Academy Award winner for production design on Raiders of the Lost Ark and for art direction on Star Wars, Reynolds was well aware of the legacy of the 1939 motion picture, The Wizard of Oz. \"Return to Oz appealed to me because it meant I would be involved in another classic. It has the same sort of magic that the first film had. It's charming, delightful and tough. Very tough.\" Tough was the operative word, not only for Reynolds, but for the creature designers, clay animation experts and other special effects professionals who worked closely with Walter Murch - some of them literally for years - to make Return to Oz. Murch was at the center of the project from the very beginning, the nucleus around which the entire production revolved. \"The whole reason that we're here is Walter Murch,\" producer Paul Maslansky said at the time. \"It was Walter's conception. He sold it to Disney. If he were a man of production background, he'd probably be producing it, too - if it wasn't too much of a titanic task just to direct it.\" Walter Murch's titanic task entailed combining two of L. Frank Baum's sequels to The Wonderful Wizard of Oz - Ozma of Oz and Land of Oz - and bringing them to life in Return to Oz. The story begins a few months after the fateful hurricane which has reduced the Gale's Kansas farm to ruins and rendered Uncle Henry a broken man. Dorothy, herself, hasn't been the same since the storm, persistently claiming to have journeyed to a faraway land full of adventure and excitement. A worried Auntie Em - in hopes of curing the girl of these delusions - takes her to the nearby Worley's Clinic, which unbeknownst to the wellintentioned woman, is run by the mysterious and sinister Doctor Worley and his weirdling head nurse. The doctor proposes to cure Dorothy with the hot new medical technology of the nineteenth century - electricity. But just as he is about to hook her up to his whirring, clicking machine, Dorothy is rescued by a mysterious little blonde girl about her age. Together, they escape from the clinic, only to find themselves in the midst of a terrible rainstorm. Dorothy sees her new friend sink into the raging waters of a rain-swollen river, but manages to escape herself by floating downstream atop her uncle's wooden chicken coop - a coop still inhabited by one of Dorothy's favorite chickens, Billina. article by The next morning, Dorothy awakens to find herself floating Brad Munson on a rapidly shrinking sea, in the midst of the Deadly Desert in the Land of Oz. She is soon stranded on the chicken coop with Billina at her side - only now Billina can talk. Before long, Dorothy and Billina find the ruins of the Yellow Brick Road - just bits of gold-colored brick - which they follow to the outskirts of a devastated Emerald City. The once magnificent municipality has been completely destroyed and is inhabited only by shrieking, squeaking 'Wheelers' - nasty humanoids with wheels instead of hands and feet. The Wheelers capture the two friends and take them to the horrible witch, Mombi, now ruler of the ravaged city. Mombi herself is only a servant of the evil and awesome Nome King, a creature of ever-changing size and shape who lives in the earth very much like a fish lives in water. It is the Nome King who has transformed Dorothy's old and dear companions, the Cowardly Lion and the Tin Man into stone statues and captured the King of Oz, the Scarecrow. Once again, Dorothy is cast in the role of reluctant heroine. She is soon befriended by a new group of brave and bizarre compatriots - Tik Tok, the wind-up mechanical man; Jack Pumpkinhead, a man of sticks with a jack-o'-lantern skull; and the strangest creature of all, the Gump, an amalgam of two sofas, some twine, a couple of palm fronds for wings and a stuffed animal head up front. Together the new friends must escape Mombi, travel to the Nome King's mountain lair, and confront him face to face. The future of the Land of Oz hangs precariously in the balance. Return to Oz may be one of the most faithful and accurate adaptations of L. Frank Baum's literary legacy ever filmed, based as it is on the sequels that immediately followed the author's original book. But it is far from the first film or stage version - the Land of Oz itself is actually one of the oldest film properties in the history of the medium. Baum wrote the children's classic in 1900, embellishing his tales of adventure and fantasy during the course of thirteen additional Oz books before his death in 1919; and author Ruth Plumley continued the tradition with yet another twenty-one books over a sixty-year period. But Baum himself saw the theatrical potential of the work. As early as 1902, Oz was produced as a musical extravaganza in Chicago, where it met with spectacular success. A year later it was a similarly huge Broadway hit. Baum then became involved in successful stagings of The Tik-Tok Man of Oz in Los Angeles in 1913, and shortly thereafter formed the Oz Film Manufacturing Company strictly to make movies of his stories. June 1914 saw the first silent film version - a five-reeler called The Patchwork Girl of Oz, which was something less than a financial success. Still, Baum went on to make The Magic Cloak of Oz, released in 1917 by the National Film Corporation, and completed His Majesty, the Scarecrow of Oz that was eventually Return to Oz photographs copyright ® 1985 by Walt Disney Productions. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Barry Peake and Richard Blanshard. Additional photographs courtesy Will Vinton Productions. Special thanks to Howard Green and Rebecca West."],["5","Return to Oz","Return To"],["6","Return to Oz","released as The New Wizard of Oz. Movie work continued after Baum's death. The Wizard of Oz was again presented as a silent film in 1925, with the Tin Man portrayed by a young Oliver Hardy, a year before the commencement of his celebrated partnership with Stan Laurel. Early in 1933, the first cartoon version was produced by Ted Eshbaugh, in full color and complete with soundtrack. It was out of this rich literary, theatrical and cinematic tradition that M-G-M created the classic musical version, The Wizard of Oz, released in 1939. And the spectre of that production haunted Return to Oz from the beginning. \"The Wizard was performed successfully on stage for almost twenty years - throughout the Tens and Twenties,\" said executive producer Gary Kurtz. \"That's where the 1939 film grew from - the vaudeville tradition of the time. It's more closely in tune with the book than the stage production, however, which deviated from it quite a bit. But vaudeville is the style they decided to use - the painted backdrops and the padded suits. The theatricality of the production was intentional, and I think it helped the film along. And yet, when The Wizard of Oz first appeared, it wasn't all that successful. It did all right, but it wasn't until it was reissued after the war, almost as a new film, that it became a major hit.\" Despite the film's eventual success, and a popularity that has literally increased with each successive generation, Walter Murch and his creative team were not interested in making its sequel. \"A film reflects the time in which it is made,\" Kurtz commented, \"and The Wizard of Oz was made in the late Thirties, reflecting an attitude that is impossible to imitate now. We certainly didn't want to try and compete with that. We wanted to do other stories that Baum had written. Return to Oz isn't a musical, and it's not in the vaudeville tradition. It's more in the spirit of the original model - a fantasy-adventure. If anything, it is just a telling of the stories in the books -a sequel to the 'Wizard of Oz' book, not the film. \" There are some minor concessions to the Judy Garland classic, however. One is that Dorothy has braids rather than a hairstyle resembling the original John R. Neill illustrations. \"And,\" added Kurtz, \"we have the ruby slippers as in the '39 film. The slippers in the original book are silver, but the M-G-M people changed them to ruby so they would show up better in color. And we even thought about that for a long time.\" Murch's rendition is not even the first effort by Disney to enter Baum's universe - with Walt Disney buying the rights to twelve of Baum's books during the 1950s. \"Disney did attempt to start an Oz film,\" said Kurtz. \"It was going to be a musical, and they even wrote several songs for it. The concept started as a television production for the Disneyland television show, but it kind of grew. They got to be afraid of the amount of money tied up in it, and so the idea evolved to make a feature (announced as The Rainbow Road to Oz in 1957). But they just got disenchanted with the screenplay. Eventually they did two of the songs from it on the Disneyland fourth anniversary show, but 6 - CINEFEX 22"],["7","Return to Oz","Walt wasn't able to do the film he wanted to do. He was never completely happy with the script.\" Productions from other sources followed instead, including one created by NBC, as well as a full-length movie and a cartoon. But none ignited the spark of the 1939 classic except for the all-black Broadway musical, The Wiz, which was released as a less-than-successful feature film by Universal Pictures in 1978. A full and complex history, to say the least, but none of it deterred Walter Murch. \"Murch had been approached to write something for Disney,\" Murch continued. \"One of the things the studio had that he was intrigued with was the Oz books. He wanted to make a story about Dorothy's eventual return to Oz. We talked about the possibility of getting something going beyond the actual screenplay, and so Walter and co-screenwriter Gill Dennis continued to work on it while I was finishing up The Dark Crystal.\" It was two years before the project got off the ground. \"We went through a long development period,\" Kurtz said, \"particularly to develop the creatures. Also, the studio passed back and forth over it a couple of times - they weren't sure they wanted to go ahead because of the cost. At one point, they decided they didn't want to; and then they changed their minds again. Then they moved the budget around, and eventually we started shooting in February 1984.\" However, Return to Oz did not take advantage of Disney's extensive Burbank facilities. Instead, Walter Murch's great undertaking would be completed half a world away at EMI Elstree - a studio complex outside London with which Gary Kurtz was intimately familiar, having spent long months there overseeing the productions of Star Wars and The Empire Strikes Back. \"We thought about producing it in Burbank, and I even did some cost projections. But the problem was partially one of space and partially one of expense. The overhead of large-scale set construction is much higher in Hollywood than in England. Part of that is due to wages; but more importantly, it's the methods that are used in construction. In England, the structural part of the set is built with pipes and scaffolding. The pipe frame is just rented and then given back when you're finished. In Hollywood, the tradition has always been to use wood supports, primarily because wood is always cheap - or at least it was until a few years ago. There was no reason not to use it; and when you tore the set down, you just burned it. But the massive amount of structural support necessary for a large set can just eat up a budget. We estimated that probably over sixty percent of our total art department budget would have been for the structural set, rather than the detail that you can actually see. I wondered, what if we could reduce that cost and put the money into the craftsmen who could produce the detail? That was the third reason we felt we wanted to make the film in England. Cabinetmakers, plasterers and sculptors are all available there in large quantity - and they do very, very fine detail.\" Finally established in the English countryside and eager to get started, Murch and Kurtz began to draw together the creative team that would ultimately bring the Neill-based designs of L. Frank Baum's immortal characters to life. To initiate the process, Gary Kurtz turned to Norman Reynolds, with whom he had worked on Star Wars and The Empire Strikes Back. With Reynolds enlisted as production designer - in charge of beginning the transition from the original Oz drawings to the finished film product - assembling the special effects task force was the next order of business. But Return to Oz' unusual storyline offered Kurtz some interesting new considerations. \"The optical and composite and matte painting sides were simpler than in most of the other pictures I'd done. On the other hand, the clay animation and mechanical creatures were much more complicated. Bringing the creatures to life involved techniques very similar to the ones we used in The Dark Crystal.\" So Dark Crystal alumni were among the first to join up. To provide the manifold - and vital - service of creature design supervisor, Gary Kurtz selected Lyle Conway who had served in like capacity on The Dark Crystal. Conway joined the team after production efforts had already begun, and was presented with designs derived by Gary Frutkoff from the original drawings in Baum's eighty-year-old books. Lastly, Murch and Kurtz agreed that the evil Nome King who not only lived inside rock, but was made of rock - could best be created through clay animation effects. Not surprisingly, Will Vinton was approached and offered the opportunity to employ his trademarked 'Claymation' process in creating the character. \"Will Vinton is kind of the father of the clay animation technique,\" Kurtz said. \"He's won several Academy and Emmy Awards for his short films. But I think this is the first time Claymation has been used in a major film, to produce a major character.\" Vinton took on the role of clay animation supervisor in August 1982, working closely with Walter Murch on plans for the Nome King and his subjects. \"We messed around a lot, just in terms of the story,\" Vinton recalled. \"The Nome King evolved through time, in a number of sessions. We thought about what we could and couldn't do - what would and wouldn't be effective. It was a lot of fun - for Walter, too - because there was time to think about it and let it evolve: whereas in the middle of production, you don't have that opportunity to roll it around in your brain. You just have to move to the next shot.\" Preliminary work began in all areas, with Murch decidedly in the center of things giving special attention not only to the clay figures, but to the mechanical creatures as well. \"One of the fascinating things about doing a film like Return to Oz,\" commented Kurtz, \"is that you start with a creature in the drawings and see it grow, through testing and functioning. But it's not until you actually photograph it in the film, in the drama of the story, that you see it come alive. And the mechanical creatures are the major characters in the story. Along with Dorothy, they are in the bulk of the film. It was a problem, but absolutely essential, to make sure that the things could act. In most cases, that required three or four performers to bring them to life, using expanded puppetry techniques, mechanicals, hydraulics, Writer-director Walter Murch coaches Fairuza Balk, the nine-year-old schoolgirl chosen to play Dorothy in Walt Disney's big-budget production of Return to Oz. Based on the second and third books in L. Frank Baum's perennially popular series, the film takes Dorothy back to the Land of Oz where she finds the Emerald City in ruins and her friends turned to stone by the nefarious Nome King. / Murch, executive producer Gary Kurtz, creature creator Lyle Conway and production designer Norman Reynolds examine miniature prototypes of the Gump - a jerry-rigged flying contraption - and Tik Tok the mechanical man. CINEFEX 22 • 7"],["8","Return to Oz","design, Murch and company based costumes employed in Instead, they turned to the original book illustrations of John R. Neill. The Cowardly Lion, for example, became an amiable quadruped. / The Tin down through the bottom of necessitating the strategic props or other characters to minimally articulated, cablecontrolled head, but relied performer to create a sense In the area of character elected not to try and duplicate the vaudevillethe 1939 M-G-M classic. Man was a mechanical construct worn by a midget - whose legs, unfortunately, extended the barrel-like torso, placement of foreground conceal them. / The Scarecrow employed a primarily on the limber movements of its human 8 • CINEFEX 22 radio control and some marionette type of work. In most cases, we had to build four or five or six different versions for various special uses. There was no way that one unit could contain all the possible ramifications of the creature.\" Norman Reynolds worked very closely with Walter Murch during preproduction, providing the director with small cardboard scale models of the sets to assist in working out camera angles. The two men would then confer, and - provided there were no modifications or script-dictated changes - the production designer could then begin preparing detailed working drawings. But Reynolds' job was far from over at that point. \"There are always problems that come up during the transition from the sketch to the working drawing and from the working drawing to the actual set. The heights, the proportions, the camera angles, the architectural features, the aesthetic feeling - those things can be the making or breaking of a set. Sketches for the director are one thing, but to actually transpose them into something you can achieve and put on screen is what's important. It's really not a matter of wishing and drawing and walking away. It's what develops as you go along. It's what you can achieve the day you actually record on film.\" Reynolds' staff changed size and personnel throughout the film. For most of the project, he worked with two art directors - Charles Bishop and Fred Hole - set decorator Michael Ford, and four draftsmen under the supervision of Reg Bream. In the beginning, however, a great deal of Reynolds' work involved finding, securing and modifying locations. \"Saudi Arabia, Rome, Spain, Kansas - we actually found all the locations,\" Reynolds recalled. \"We had planned to go to Sardinia and Algeria for the sequence involving the Deadly Desert. We were also going to a place called Ciudad Encantada - the Enchanted City - north of Madrid, in Spain. It's a series of huge, monolithic rocks which we were going to use for a sequence where Tik Tok and Dorothy are trapped by the Wheelers. We were actually going to shoot in Kansas for two weeks at one point - that would have been lovely.\" The Nome King's throne room was going to be shot in the palace at Kisserta, near Naples, while Hadrian's villa outside Rome was set for Mombi's palace and the ruins of the Emerald Plans for extensive location shooting were, however, not to be realized. Along with other preproduction efforts, Norman Reynolds' exotic settings fell victim to a radical financial reevaluation which was meanwhile taking place at Disney's Burbank headquarters. Producer Paul Maslansky - who had previously worked on other pictures involving special effects, including The Long Ships, The Blue Birdand Damnation Alley - was called in to assess the situation. \"At a certain point, Disney was not of a mind to continue even though they had spent quite a bit of money developing the project. They wanted to have certain assurances that the picture wouldn't be one of those open holes into which they would pour money. There had to be certain restraints. Dick Berger (then studio president), with whom I had worked at Fox many times in this situation, called me and asked if I would go over to England just to take a look. They wanted an overview to see if it would be possible to confine the budget for the entire film to $25 million. The picture had reached a point where there wasn't any clearly defined budget - at least not one they could live with. It's part of my background to do this sort of thing - I've done it before, many times - so I gave them a hard appraisal.\" Part of Maslansky's 'hard appraisal' involved the special effects budget - understanding it, organizing it and getting it completely under control. For help, Maslansky turned to an old and close friend -Zoran Perisic, a Yugoslavian-born special effects professional who was rostrum cameraman for 2001: A Space Odyssey, a visual effects consultant for the Superman movies and inventor of the Zoptic front projection system. Maslansky and Perisic first met on Irwin Allen's The Long Ships in 1961, and over the next few years had stayed in touch Ship in 901. and on an irregular basis. But Maslansky was more than a little surprised when he learned of Perisic's involvement with 2001. \"The next thing I knew, there he was getting the Academy Award for Superman - and then another one. So as soon as I saw the demands of this picture - especially the flying sequences - I couldn't think of a better person to do it than Zoran. \" With Gary Kurtz' enthusiastic approval, Perisic was brought on as special effects consultant for Oz. \"Paul Maslansky called me and asked if I would like to read the script, to see if there was anything we could help him out on,\" Perisic recalled. \"I foolishly said, 'Oh, I'll read it over tonight and give you a rough idea of the breakdown in the morning.' I took this script, read it that evening and thought to myself: 'My gosh, this is colossal. Just monumental.' Up 'til then, I don't think anybody really knew exactly what they were into. It was a massive operation optically, and yet everybody was concentrating on the mechanics of the thing - which were important also, but the opticals were being neglected.\" Perisic was assigned the duty of producing an exacting analysis of what special effects would be needed on a scene-by-scene, shot-by-shot basis. \"My first few weeks were spent getting with Walter and sorting out the storyboards to determine exactly what was needed. We had to thrash them out, going sequence by sequence, to get a thoroughly detailed list of every scene and all the possible alternatives for doing it. Then we chose the one approach that he liked.\" Shooting actually began before Perisic had put the final touches on that breakdown. \"But at that point, it was possible to know exactly how many optical shots there were - how manv bipacks. how manv optical printer composites, et cetera. Based on that, we could calculate the cost - how much cost there would be in camera work, how much in Claymation, and the rest.\" Some concerns were expressed about producing the opticals in Great Britain - rooted in the long-standing belief that England produced inferior work in the field. Neither Gary Kurtz nor Perisic gave that sentiment much credence. \"What I've found in my own experience, both in Hollywood and England, is that any"],["9","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 9"],["10","Return to Oz","10 • CINEFEX 22 Lir SERI PRIL"],["11","Return to Oz","disagreement regarding the quality of workmanship is due not to the equipment but to the time available,\" Kurtz said. \"If you're not willing to commit the time to do the thing over - since very few of those shots come out perfect the first time - something is generally wrong. It is true that there are very few of the integrated shops similar to ILM in England. Each of the commercial houses are geared to something special - and you have to go to several different places.\" The final decision was to parcel out the effects work to a variety of small effects companies. \"When I started,\" Perisic said, \"the prevailing thought was to make a deal with one specific house - in fact, there were several houses that were after the job. They all said they could do the entire picture, and each wanted a colossal amount of money. The problem was in having a project that was actually larger and more complex than any single house could manage. My suggestion to Paul Maslansky was simply to field work to the appropriate person at the appropriate time. So we established arrangements with a number of people. We'd say, 'Okay, we're going to be giving you some things; and if you're interested, give us your prices to do it right. Since we had every sequence broken down, we knew exactly how many shots there were, and exactly how they should go. Then we'd get a fixed price. Any other way could have taken forever and still have cost an indefinite amount of money.\" Money, after all, was at the core of the Oz project - the goal being to spend as little as possible and yet still create effects of acceptably high standard. Perisic felt that parceling out work to a variety of houses actually enhanced the quality. \"These guys knew that if they were going to get the next shot, they were going to have to deliver the first one, and deliver it well and on time. I didn't want to give it to people who would farm it out to someone else and then charge us. I might as well send it somewhere else myself. The way I did it was to have the people directly responsible for what they were doing; and if they weren't, then they just didn't get the next job. I think that was fairer, and it gave us a chance to use different people who were good at doing that particular kind of work.\" And so, Perisic's meetings with Murch and outside vendors produced the much-needed effects breakdown. Based on this, a firm budget could be estimated. With a final price tag projected at a little under $25 million - entailing a hefty $5 million cut - and armed with this new information, Disney executives gave Return to Oz the hoped-for authorization to proceed. \"About twenty percent of the entire budget is special effects - maybe not quite that much,\" said Gary Kurtz. \"A million dollars on the creatures, about two million on the Claymation and opticals work - it's pretty hard to break it down between them - probably about $500,000 on the miniatures and matte paintings: and mechanical effects probably up around two million.\" Ultimately, that budget had as much effect on the final look of the film as Walter Murch, L. Frank Baum and state-of-the-art technologies combined. Among the first to rebound from Disney's jarring financial reassessment was Norman Reynolds, who altered his set designs from preferred locations to the inevitable stage. \"The picture evolved from seventy-five percent locations and twenty-five percent on stages and sets to completely the other way around. Actually eighty percent of it was on stage and twenty percent was on location in England. We even did Kansas in England.\" The great Salisbury Plains, near Stonehenge, doubled for midwestern America in the film's early Kansas sequences; and Worley's Clinic, visible in the first few scenes, was built as a set with a typical turn-of-the-century interior. And rather than sending the whole crew to Hadrian's villa near Rome, the ruins of Oz would be produced in miniature for use in the flying sequences, and in full-size sets only as needed. \"What we built,\" Reynolds said, \"was just enough to make the sequence work - the area where Dorothy enters and gets grabbed by the Wheelers, and that's it. While it's laid out as part of a city, it's really a series of pieces that facilitate the action. It could have been a great deal larger and more complex, but to build it on any scale could have taken six months. We had to make the best of it in the time we had.\" Expensive company sojourns to Sardinia and Algeria for the Deadly Desert sequences were cut as well, with only a plate photography crew dispatched to those exotic locales. The flying would be done using bluescreen or front projection, and for the scenes where Dorothy and Tik Tok were to be trapped in a gully near Cuidad Encantada, they would instead be trapped in an alley on a set. \"The script changed and bent slightly to make it work, but the story's so strong I don't think it hurt at all,\" commented Reynolds. Still, the confines of a stage posed certain limitations that Reynolds and others regretted. \"A set can't really compete with reality. You can achieve things with special effects and opticals and models, but you can't beat the real thing. I always like to think that sets should be used only when there's no other way of doing it - the end of the line, rather than the beginning of the line. And there are a couple of places, like Hadrian's villa, that I'd love to shoot. Of course, all movies go through this. They start out with something and more or less assume their personalities as they go along, out of necessity and Necessity and invention also dictated the ingenuity of the special effects department, particularly in one of its earliest and most adventurous applications - the use of clay animation to create the Nome King and his subjects. \"The characters are in the books,\" Kurtz said, \"but they're portrayed like traditional fantasy gnomes - kind of small, squat little creatures with funny heads. We wanted to try something different - something that was more interesting than the Munchkin idea of getting a lot of little people and putting them in funny suits.\" The result was a group of creatures made of stone - who actually lived in stone. And Will Vinton's Claymation process of using plasticene as the basic medium of animation - as opposed to the more widespread stop-motion technique of using armatured foam rubber puppets- seemed the most appropriate technology for bringing the Nomes and their king to life. \"The Fabric designer Lesja Liber assists performer John Alexander into his Cowardly Lion suit. To obtain an appropriate leonine stance, the legs were constructed in such a way that Alexander - walking around on all fours - had to use mechanical arm extenders in the front while standing on tiptoe in the back. The head - relatively lightweight for its size - was articulated with small servomotors. / Lyle Conway adds finishing touches to his Scarecrow sculpture. / A large-scale miniature of the restored Emerald City, with accompanying painted backdrop. CINEFEX 22 • 11"],["12","Return to Oz","Dorothy's return to Oz by a chicken proved to be one of assignments for the film's animatronics staff. / One of the early proposals was to produce a larger-than-life bird and photograph it with oversized settings and props Conway and crew were able into an actual-size chicken figure. / Billina's lifelike coat was produced by Val Jones, loops of wool to which real charge of the overall Billina Toto remains behind in Kansas, replaced during talking chicken named Billina. Creating a convincing mechanical the more challenging - an idea which was ultimately discarded as impractical. In the end, to cram the necessary articulating mechanisms employing a hand-woven elastic fabric laced with chicken feathers were painstakingly glued. / Stephen Norrington - at work on one of the mechanical legs - was in construction project. 12 • CINEFEX 22 Nomes went through a lot of different phases,\" Vinton recalled, \"beginning with a sort of cherub-like creature, very close to the original Baum illustrations - almost egg-shaped characters with very long beards. Though Walter wanted to stick very closely to the original illustrations with most of the imagery, the Nomes were the antagonists of the story, and he wanted them to be something a little bit less fairy-like - more threatening, I think. A little wilder. And this whole notion of bringing rock to life is almost a contradiction in terms. One of the first things we dealt with all along was, 'What is the nature of rockiness?' - and one of the primary answers was that rock doesn't move. Yet the whole thing was to get that rock to move. The idea was that Nomes were in this kind of plasma of rock, a sea of rock.\" Efforts to develop the characterizations for these incredible creatures began in August 1982, with Walter Murch and Will Vinton in close cooperation. Working through five drafts of the script - and dealing particularly in the area of conceptualization - Murch, Vinton and production chief character designer, Barry Bruce, eventually came up with a set of drawings and sculptures based on Nicol Williamson, the actor engaged to portray the wicked king. The team developed the rock extensions' of Williamson into a final look for the king and his subjects. Will Vinton also traveled from his studio in Portland, Oregon, to the soundstages in London to participate in principal photography; and based on the plates that were created there, he and his crew began to render the scenes and backgrounds that would eventually be part of the illusion of Claymation. Many of Vinton's animators, machinists, editors, electronics personnel and other support staff members were just completing principal photography on a complex and challenging full-length Claymation feature, The Adventures of Mark Twain. \"Twain was just going into postproduction,\" Vinton said. \"It had a mixture of effects like those used in regular live-action films - seventysome composite shots, two hundred or so front screen projection shots, upwards of fifty relatively complex optical composite shots using frontlight/backlight generated mattes, and more.\" And the lessons learned on Twain, concerning the integration of effects in stop-motion animation, became immediately applicaOne of those lessons involved the importance of complete reference film. To capture the realistic movements and subtlety of gesture, Twain had actually been shot and edited as a liveaction film before Claymation ever began. The same reliance on live-action footage derived from principal photography would hold true on Oz. \"Normally,\" said Vinton, \"we work from a reference film of actors going through lines - not in the sense of rotoscoping, but going for the little nuances of expression, the mannerisms that an actor puts into a performance. And 1 didn't see this as being any different. We took our cues from Nicol Williamson - his mannerisms, his portrayal - but it had to be interpreted in terms of rockiness. At first we tried to alter the reference film itself as part of the design process. \"We tried a"],["13","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 13 medica lex rapid"],["14","Return to Oz","14 • CINEFEX 22"],["15","Return to Oz","lot of experimentation with reflective mylar - the actors doing their lines while being photographed in mylar that distorted their face and hand movements. It was an interesting technique, and some of the tests were marvelous, but they weren't rocky. They were fluid. The changes and distortions that happened to the faces weren't created by the lines or the performance themselves; they were random and tended to destroy what was a good performance. So we dropped back to a basic attitude - you take your cues strictly from the actors themselves.\" With the help of Claymation producer David Altschul, the crew at Vinton's studio was split into ten camera units, and work on the design and construction of the miniature sets began. Machinists created armatures for the various Nome King models which would be used to represent the villainous character during the different phases of his transition. Bases were made of plywood for the beginning of the Nome King's metamorphosis - when flat walls or humped shapes were called for - while metal was used for the sometimes fully articulated, movable machine armatures of the models' later stages. After the bases were constructed, the next step was to create textures for the Claymation miniatures that would look like real rock, dirt and stone - with the desired result being a sandstone-like substance that seemed like rock but was still malleable. \"We went through months of testing clay-plasticene aggregates,\" Vinton said, \"working with lots of oil-based clays from different manufacturers, because different companies' materials have different consistencies. In the end we used types that had a wax base and could melt. We heated them and mixed them like paints, using a big double boiler until it was like pea soup. Then we stirred in color pigments - sometimes universal colorant, which is used for standard oil-based paint, sometimes tempera, sometimes just oil-based artists' paints. In some cases, it was actually a matter of toning down the range normally available to clay animation artists. We've always been spoiled by the total spectrum of possible colors. This time, though, we really needed to match exactly the rock parts that were in the live-action set. And texture was even more important. Partially, we sculpted in the texture using sandpaper, tipped tools and jagged rocks attached to normal sculpting tools in order to make depressions and scrapes. But we needed something more than that, especially for the miniatures, to get closeups of the material itself. Even with all the modeled colors, it needed something more. \"We ended up experimenting with a lot of different kinds of aggregates actually added while the clay was in the molten stage-sand, small bits of rock, crushed walnut shells. We would spray the walnut shells various colors to get them to look like gravel within rock. We came up with a pretty nice spectrum of rock material - actually a wider range than we needed because some of the sets that Norman Reynolds came up with are quite sculptural - man-made walls. We actually backed off the realistic aggregate for some of those sequences.\" Developing the character, and giving that character attributes found only in a world of pure fantasy and invention included ordaining the Nome King with the ability to change his own form. As he gains more control over others - by turning them into gewgaws and trinkets for his ornament room - the Nome King becomes more and more human. \"It's a pretty heavy-duty role,\" commented Will Vinton. \"He seemed to be able to change size and be quite magnificent and huge when he wanted to, and yet he evolves as well - which is an interesting alteration. From crude rock stages to what's basically a real actor.\" That transformation involved extremely tight matching between live-action and Claymation - and tight referencing to the real movements of Nicol Williamson so that when the real actor appeared, the simplest gestures and facial movements would seem familiar and natural. \"Zoran Perisic and I discussed matching at great length in London,\" Vinton recalled, \"but once the principal photography was done, we were on our own. It was up to us to make sure that everything matched. The reference film footage was cut along with the rest of the picture. Then we animated just those portions that we needed. In a lot of shots, the live characters in the foreground are being shot at the same time - with the rest of the principal photography-so they intercut with the workprint very well. Actually, we developed a pretty neat little front screen projection rig that worked great for doing lineups with a live-action plate. We put the live-action plate in the front-screen projector unit and by switching back and forth between the projector light and the set light, you could basically marry the two images and make comparisons while you were building the set. Then you could see it on the videotape in the camera. That worked really well.\" The actual transition from Claymation to live-action included enormous assistance from makeup expert Robin Grantham - and it was no simple task. \"This one character had to be created in conjunction with more partners than normal,\" said Grantham. \"And the fact that the Claymation people were in Oregon while we were in the U.K. created a time-warp sometimes. Trying to imagine how people in Oregon were seeing the concept all that distance away was difficult.\" Making the transition flow smoothly required a delicate touch from the Nome King's team as well as the use of five separate stages in the transformation from stone to human form. The first three were Claymation, the last two were live-action, with the eyes becoming a focus of particular attention in the middle stages - the last Claymation version had very realistically portrayed eyes, while the first live-action mask's eyes were made to look hard and artificial. \"For the rock-like stages,\" said Grantham, \"I used prosthetic pieces - foam rubber on the cheeks and brows. I added an expensive real-hair beard, too, which I gradually covered with rock. It ended up eighty percent rock and only a little bit of hair, as though the flesh were just emerging. What we were really striving for was one of those things where the audience never knows where the change takes place.\" That meticulous attention to the detail of matching Claymation with live-action was a challenge throughout the film, and Zoran Perisic did his part to make a monumental chore look Lyle Conway demonstrates the basic Billina handpuppet configuration. Depending on which version was in use, the operator's hand entered either from the back or the underside of the bird, with two fingers extending up through the neck and into a pair of cups in the back of the skull. Essential neck and head moves were produced with these two fingers, augmented by minute cable mechanisms for eye and mouth movements. / Conway operated Billina during her first film test. With his puppeteering arm extended through a hole in the makeshift set, the creature designer was able to observe Billina's on-camera actions via a video monitor. / On the other side of the wall, Val Jones and Walter Murch prepare Billina for her screen test. CINEFEX 22 • 15"],["16","Return to Oz","Emma Ridley standing in for Dorothy, Wilson - backed up Greenaway and Geoff Felix - practices a coordinated move with the Billina puppet. / Steve Norrington and Lyle Conway examine the inner workings of a free-standing, fully-mechanized chicken puppet developed for scenes where hand operation was Yellow Brick Road, Fairuza Balk plays a scene with the operated by Wilson, with During production, Mak Wilson became Billina's principal operator. With by cable operators David impossible. / On the scattered remains of the mechanical Billina - support from Swee Lim, David Greenaway and easy. By using three different methods - closeups featuring straight Claymation, bluescreen and bipack composites - Perisic artfully blended clay animation with the live-action that surrounded it and frequently appeared with it in the same shot. Perisic chose bipacks for three reasons: cost, time, and quality - with quality being the most important consideration. \"It seems everybody thinks that if you're going to have an optical, then you just shoot this bit over here and that bit over there and you put it on an optical printer, and by some miracle it's going to come together. That's not the case. In fact, in this particular circumstance, using an optical printer would have been the wrong thing. It's almost like using a tank where a bicycle will do. And remember, you can get through certain places on a bicycle where a tank wouldn't go at all. For example, you have a cave wall where everything is rocky, and suddenly one of the rocks becomes alive. There's no point in shooting that and then trying to marry it up on an optical printer. It's very difficult to get a soft edge between the two elements, and if you're reducing one of the images, you usually get the perspective all screwed up. It's best if you just give them a print with both elements in exact positions and marry them up before the Claymation starts.\" That matching took careful planning. \"In the very beginning my biggest worry was that the texture of clay and the texture of the actual live-action wasn't going to work very well. In going through the storyboards with Walter, we tried to sort out shots where you could introduce camera movement as much as possible so it didn't look like it was all clay. If you can track and then freeze, and then clay begins, it's a better shot than if you're just standing there all the time.\" Originally, Vinton's team in Portland was going to reproduce the entire live-action frame - duplicating whatever Perisic and crew shot. \"They thought that if things didn't line up correctly, there would be room on the optical printer to just move it up or down or sideways until it caught. That's a fallacy. If you move it around, you enlarge the image - so the composition changes altogether. Finally, after a couple of tests, I managed to convince everybody that the best way was to use bipack compositing.\" Perisic's bipack compositing involved shooting a scene in the usual way, making a positive from it, and then making a registered guide-print in normal color. This guide-print was then sent to Vinton in Portland - or Vinton would carry it himself, since he made frequent trips to Elstree when the live-action sequences were being filmed. Then, if a Claymation character was to appear exclusively in the top right-hand corner of the frame, for instance, the clay animators needed to duplicate only that portion of the set in terms of texture and color. \"You start by making that part of the wall look exactly the same as it does on the live-action,\" Perisic explained, \"and then you carry on with the clay animation. The rest of the frame is black. \" In the final compositing, Perisic made sure that the two interpositives that were being combined were precisely timed: so very little correction or variation was necessary. \"The whole trick was to use this soft-edge matting method,\" he continued. \"And you can do that on a bipack camera more easily because you're using a normal lens against a printer light. You can put your mask as close to the lens as you like, and then choose the stop that will give you a wide area of joining. The left and right elements tend to bleed into each other then - they overlap. And the join between Claymation and live-action that you'd expect to see isn't there, really. Right at that point, fifty percent of it is Claymation and fifty percent of it is live-action.\" Perisic further refined the process by visiting the Portland facility and helping to rig a projector system that would allow the animators to mask off the portion of the projected picture that would not be animated, and still have a perfectly lined-up angle for construction and shooting. \"It was a perfect blend,\" he said. \"There is no one point where the real set element finishes and the Claymation set starts. It's so soft that it just merges.\" With the human heroine, Fairuza Balk, in front of the camera, and the inhuman villain under construction in Portland, attention turned to the design, construction and operation of Dorothy's other friends and enemies - the creatures that populated and supported virtually every scene in the film. Unfortunately, however, when the script was revised to accommodate the streamlined budget, in addition to Norman Reynolds' diverse settings, some of the fantasy characters were affected as well. As a result, Dorothy's oldest and dearest companions - the Cowardly Lion, the Scarecrow and the Tin Man - are only visible in a few scenes at the very end of Return to Oz, victims of the financial cutbacks that turned them to stone almost as literally as the Nome King did. One manifestation of this cutback involved a modified version of the Cowardly Lion, which, unlike Bert Lahr's classic humanoid interpretation, actually walks on all fours. But even though this more recent rendition of the character is decidedly less like a man than its predecessor, care was taken to avoid too much realism. In fact, the first prototype of the lion possessed a greater similarity to its jungle-prowling inspiration than director Murch cared to see. \"Walter wanted something like the big dog character on Sesame Street-something that would walk around on all fours,\" Lyle Conway commented. Yet, as a fantasy creation, the Cowardly Lion possessed a personality unique unto himself. So mechanical designer John Stephenson went to work combining the requisite anthropomorphic qualities with a beastlike posture. To make the quadruped stance convincing, tensile steel arm extensions - similar to the heavy-duty ones developed for the apes in Greystoke - were added to the body suit, extending from handgrips to about a foot farther down. The paws laid flat on the ground, but the mechanism itself allowed a certain amount of movement; so if it came in contact with an object, the limb seemed to react in a lifelike fashion. The suit itself was primarily the work of Cas Willing - with help from Greystoke alumnus, fabric designer Val Jones -and was made of lycra with padded foam muscles and a foam chest. \"The whole thing zipped up with a harness inside,\" explained Jones. \"It was"],["17","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 17"],["18","Return to Oz","18 • CINEFEX 22"],["19","Return to Oz","covered with a material that's actually smoother than real fur, and only slightly less realistic. But that's what Walter wanted - much more of a character.\" John Alexander, the man who portrayed the Cowardly Lion in its few appearances, actually had to stand on tiptoe because the back feet of his costume were built up to enhance the feline's haunch-shape. Stephenson tried to make it as comfortable as possible for Alexander by installing supports customized especially to fit the performer's foot. But then, Alexander - who was one of the apes in Greystoke - was more than familiar with the various annoyances that result from donning an animal's skin. \"John is incredible,\" said creature choreographer Pons Maar, \"I don't think anyone else could have been in that suit and done what he's done with it - taking the straightforward look of how lions in nature move and transferring it into the costume.\" The costume tended to be rather heavy by nature, and care was taken to eliminate excess weight at every opportunity. Keeping this in mind, as well as Murch's dictum to avoid a ferocious-looking beast, John Stephenson and crew went through an involved evolutionary process in designing the lion's skull. What began as a realistic and somewhat frightening model eventually took on the unimposing visage of a friendlier beast. Sculpted by Steve Martin - who worked on the apes in Greystoke and was responsible for the wigs and hair on The Dark Crystal - the lion's head was never actually designed for some of the in-frame closeups that became necessary, but the finished product was successful nonetheless. \"From the original skull, we changed the whole forehead area to make it much higher,\" said Stephenson. \"Now, he's got a frown, and is slightly heavy-lidded, so he looks just a little worried most of the time. The major consideration was that the head had to be extremely light, which was a major problem. The skull is actually very close to a real lion's skull. It's made out of Kevlar, which is lighter and stronger than fiberglass - it's the stuff they make racing boats out of, in fact.\" The Kevlar cut a substantial amount of weight, as did the frame which was made from pierced aluminum. Further weight was eliminated by making the teeth from fiberglass instead of the usual, heavier, dental acrylic. The whole affair was then built on the front of a helmet which was tied to John Alexander's shoulders with a strong elastic strap in order to transfer the weight from his neck to his shoulders. The actor's head ended up far to the rear of the lion head, just below the mane, with the model's limited articulations operated by radio control. Further ballast was avoided when only the small servomotors for the eyes were mounted inside the head. The bigger, more powerful servo for the mouth was worn on the chest, installed in the rib-cage compartment of the lycra body suit. The servos in the skull controlled the movement of the eyeballs - one on the left and one on the right - while another mounted further forward operated the blink with a simple on/off switch. The eyes were kept aligned with a special bar that functioned like the axle of a car. \"And there was a facility for actually focusing them,\" added Stephenson. \"We arranged it so that if the lion was looking at something very close to him, we could focus the eyes by twiddling a little knob. Open out the gauges and he'd be looking into the distance.\" Even the eyes themselves were made lighter than usual. \"Ordinarily when you make eyes, you make a core and then fill it up with resin. We didn't do that with these. We just used a plastic shell. Basically what you do is make a donut of color in painted acrylic, and you drop that into your shell. You then fill up with white acrylic behind it until it's covered. Then you paint the back of it black, which gives you the depth for the pupil because it's slightly behind the color then. That was good enough - and light enough, which is the main In spite of the meticulous care given to creating the Cowardly Lion, the character did not weather the turbulence of Disney's budget-cutting storm very well. \"When the film was cut,\" John Stephenson said, \"the first thing they did was cut the lion out of it. Originally, we were going to do three heads - a closeup head, a light medium-shot head the only one finally produced) and then a very light stunt head. After the cut, they wouldn't allow me to do a closeup head, which is what I really wanted to do more than anything else. Also, they wouldn't allow us to make a duplicate costume. There are spare legs and feet, but only one Yet for all of the limitations imposed by the film's financial circumstances, the Cowardly Lion survives the finished edition of Return to Oz in far better shape than his old friends, the Scarecrow and the Tin Man. The Scarecrow, for instance, was little more than a minimally articulated, cable-operated head that relied almost entirely on the movements of an agile and incredibly limber performer, Justin Case, for its realism. But Pons Maar, who designed and coordinated the maneuvers for all of the various residents of Oz, had something in mind for Case other than a typical, floppy stumble. \"Almost all my work was to develop a graceful look, so he's not just a very limber-legged character, not a monster who happens to have limber legs. He is made of straw and, of course, he's very light and his limbs get away from him - but the Scarecrow is the king too. So if a limb does get away from him, it gets away in a very graceful way. He is also uncommonly at ease with his boneless grace. He doesn't look at his leg and go: 'Oh! I'm falling!' It's just the way he is. When you open a door and walk through, you don't look back and say: 'Oh! I've gone through a door!' It's just second nature. So I've taken that quality - keeping the limberness in the body, but giving him a bit of regalness.\" Although Justin Case and Pons Maar rallied nobly by providing the spirit of the character, Lyle Conway was less than satisfied with the final product. \"It's one of those things that for a whole bunch of reasons, just didn't come together. It was only going to be in briefly at the end of the film, and everybody said we didn't have to do an overly mechanized thing. Then all of a sudden it was turning up in quite a few of the big scenes. There are these lingering closeups on the head that don't work - it doesn't do On their quest, Dorothy and Billina encounter Tik Tok - the Royal Army of Oz - a patented clockwork mechanical man, guaranteed to work perfectly for a thousand years, but whose various functions are interrupted with troublesome regularity whenever his wind-up keys run down. Together, the three present themselves to Mombi in the great mirrored sitting room of her opulent palace. / Mak Wilson models a plexiglass sphere which served as the prototype for Tik Tok's rotund body. / During production, the mechanical man was played by Michael Sundin, an actor with acrobatic experience who contorted himself into Tik Tok's cramped torso area for as long as an hour at a time. Though Sundin was able to walk and provide minimal arm movement for the character, head and facial articulations were achieved externally via radio control. CINEFEX 22 • 19"],["20","Return to Oz","Billina peers out from the eye of Jack Pumpkinhead - another Oz inhabitant who journey. / Chris Ostwald, who was in charge of the examines the squash and stretch' mechanism whereby Jack's facial articulations were made possible. / Jack is momentarily separated when the Gump falls apart Dorothy and her friends near the top of the Nome King's mountain. When the head lands upside down on his attached to a cherry picker was used to lower the head controlled puppet operated from beneath the elevated Dorothy ponder a way out of the tower room in Mombi's joins Dorothy on her character's construction, from his pumpkin head in midair and deposits catches up with Jack, it neck. / On stage, a cable onto Jack's body - a rodset. / 'Floppy Jack' and palace. 20 • CINEFEX 22 anything! I'm embarrassed by that.\" And then there is the particularly uncomfortable matter of the Tin Man - a character whose design is based very closely on John Neill's original drawings of the strange creature with spindly legs, a pail for a head, and a funnel for a hat - making it virtually impossible to be constructed in such a way that a normal-size actor could be placed inside. The original idea had been to employ the use of opticals and a marionette-style een to eut this approach pro ering c puppet, but this approach proved too complicated and expensive. So the crew was left with trying to concoct a Tin Man that would carry a small person in its relatively little belly, and still be able to walk and perform at least some basic movements. It was not a major success. The small person - movie veteran Deep Roy - operated the head from a rod inside the torso, turning it left or right as necessary. Minimal facial features were handled by radio-controlled servos that operated the eyes, eyebrows and mouth. The performer also managed to manipulate one arm from inside, but there was little room for anything else - in fact, there was so little room that the operator's legs actually hung down, out of the body and in plain view. To combat this minor difficulty, diversionary tactics were employed; and in the few shots where the Tin Man ultimately appears, he is constantly blocked by another character in the foreground, so the dangling legs of the actor cannot be seen. While the creative staff may have suffered their share of disappointment over the less-than-successful Tin Man, Scarecrow and Cowardly Lion, the invention of Dorothy's current sidekicks would prove to be infinitely more satisfying - with the help of an ample budget for the animatronics workshop. Oddly enough, the creature that posed the greatest challenge for the crew wasn't a fanciful creation at all. It was just an ordinary Plymouth Rock hen named Billina - who could talk. Billina's commonness was, in fact, the very essence of the quandary. Creating a believable chicken of actual size that could blink, move, walk and talk convincingly was no easy task. \"People know what a chicken looks like,\" lamented Lyle Conway. Coming up with a workable concept was the first step in the arduous process of hatching the Billina character. \"Originally,\" Conway said, \"the notion was to build oversized sets for a larger-than-life version, then drop that into the picture somehow. I had this image of a massive rubber chicken - a Rodan sort of thing.\" That idea quickly passed in favor of a series of actual-size mechanical birds that could be manipulated by an operator's hand inside the body and further articulated with radio control and cables. \"There are several versions,\" explained Mak Wilson, Billina's principal operator. \"On one, my hand went up through the bottom of the body. On another it went through the back. Then there was a radio-controlled chicken that stood free. I had to make my hand as small as possible to fit up inside the neck, with my index finger and the finger next to it fitting into the cups at the back of the chicken's skull. The head movement is done with just those two fingers. Coming down the neck are cables that go to a control joystick, which - depending"],["21","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 21"],["22","Return to Oz","22 • CINEFEX 22"],["23","Return to Oz","on the situation - I usually had strapped to a belt. That joystick controlled the direction of the head - sideways, up and down - and there was a button on it which controlled the mouth.\" Lyle Conway sculpted a number of chicken legs and feet - some cable-controlled and some devised as flexible wire armatures - that could be detached and changed depending on Billina's role in a particular shot. A bladder in the neck provided a breathing function which was controlled by another operator, and she was supplied with fully movable wings which - depending on the shot - were operated by hidden bladders or with rods that could be framed out of camera range. The mechanism that provided the breathing function was brass, the wires were made of dacron for strength and durability, and the whole affair was covered by a remarkable woven and feathered fabric. Billina's feathered coat was actually a double-layered fabric devised by Val Jones and knitted on her own weaving machine. The woven, elastic-thread fabric admitted loops of wool which were then cut open to create a realistic pile, resembling a carpet. The chicken feathers were then glued on by hand, one at a time, to develop a layered, feathered cloth that would move as the chicken moved, very much like real skin. A furry version of the same material was used in the head and body suit for the Cowardly Lion, but the half dozen versions of Billina were far more time-consuming. \"It took about a week just to make a neck,\" explained Jones. \"A whole chicken took about three weeks.\" With each feather individually inserted, the work could become understandably tedious, and an assistant was brought in to help during the final stages. Maintenance of the material was a consideration, too. \"It's quite fragile, but actually the fabric stood up to an amazing amount of wear. Still, they're hell to keep on the set, especially where you've got debris falling or something that makes them tatty. It's quite difficult to keep them looking smart.\" The job of actually building the bird fell to Stephen Norrington, a nineteen-year-old prodigy who had made his mark while working on Greystoke. \"When I came on, right after the start of the film, Lyle already had this amazing idea of a sophisticated hand puppet. For three or four months, we sort of played around with grand ideas. 'What's this chicken going to do?' we asked. 'It's going to do everything, ' was the response.\" The first hen built was a prototype to determine if the assorted parts were going to be in practical, usable positions. The second was a full-fledged working model, followed by a total of seven chicken heads and main parts that would be used, reused and modified as Billina's role in the film developed. The main construction challenge was posed by the minute size of the creature - particularly the chicken's tiny head. \"Lyle first sculpted one head,\" Norrington said, \"and I had to try to fit the mechanism in. When that didn't work, he went back and did it again to get it right.\" From this collaboration, a very thin plastic shell was devised to serve as Billina's skull. A miniature cable mechanism caused the acetate eyelids to blink on both sides, and since only a very small servomotor could be installed within the miniature head, the movement of the eyes was reduced considerably from the modelmakers' initial plans. \"Originally, we were going to have them move all around, but the size of the head just didn't allow us to do that. We ended up having them go side to side, diagonally, to give her more expression.\" Billina eventually became a collection of more than twenty moving parts, including a variety of interchangeable feet that could tuck up under her, let her lay down, scratch the ground, and much more - but with the completion of the model, a new generation of problems was in the offing. The difficulties began with, of course, the operation of Billina - a complicated duty that never involved less than four or five people. \"I controlled the actual body movement and head,\" said Mak Wilson, \"and during rehearsal, I did the voice as well. We actually had an additional five puppeteers though. Somebody did the wings, another person did the blinking, another person had the legs and someone else was on the tail. It was quite complicated, and it did take quite a few weeks to get the coordination between us.\" Developing the basic movement was the first item on the agenda. Wilson spent some time watching real chickens, including the ones kept on the Kansas set and some in a yard across the way from his home. \"There are some things they do which are very difficult to duplicate - the scratching, the scrape. It looks easy when you see it, but actually to imitate that! And the walking. The way they walk is with the body traveling first, and the neck is so fluid that it gets left behind and actually has to catch up. The move is so difficult, in fact, both in terms of operation and keeping the puppeteer's hand out of frame - that in the film you mostly see it talking, standing and sitting still, though we did do some jumping and flying into position. Those are the fun ones. It's quite sad, in a way, but most people don't really know how a chicken moves. There are very few people - apart from country people - who might see a real live chicken and know how it moves. We're hoping, partly through that ignorance, to be able to fool the audience.\" The mechanics of dealing with the demands of various shots brought new challenges for puppeteers and camera crew alike. The complications arising from the efforts to coordinate Billina's moves and align camera angles seemed endless, and frequently called for valor above and beyond the call of duty. \"Sometimes,\" continued Wilson, \"we were literally behind the chicken, which was difficult to do when we were on ground level. We had to get down very low. We only had a few shots where we could come up through the floor (in fact, only two sets were built on raised platforms to allow operation of any of the characters from beneath), so most of the time she's standing on something - and I'm just going through the back, or from underneath.\" These inconveniences didn't keep Wilson and his stalwart company from attempting some of the more challenging moves, however - like using a set of the mechanical legs and some careful framing of the shot to get Billina to fly up and land with her talons gripping. \"We were trying to have her fly up, land, regain her balance, flutter, scratch her face with one of her 'Trolley Jack' was a fullymechanized puppet employed for waist-up shots. Mounted on a four-wheeled cart for mobility, 'Trolley Jack' was operated primarily by Brian Henson who controlled the head and body movements with a single rod mechanism which extended down from the base of the puppet. Other puppeteers assisted with facial articulations. / In situations where neither puppet would suffice, a third variation of the Jack Pumpkinhead character was devised - with a human operator, Stewart Larange, inside. An early test for the CINEFEX 22 • 23"],["24","Return to Oz","friendly. In the ruins of the Emerald City - a Norman Reynolds set constructed on the EMI Elstree backlot - screaming Wheelers. / John Stephenson engineered the mechanism which allowed the Wheelers - who have wheels instead of hands and feet - to perform the kinds of complex actions called for in the script. / Dorothy flees played by Pons Maar, who also served as movement various fantasy creatures found within the Land of Oz. Not all of the characters Dorothy encounters are Dorothy and Tik Tok are accosted by a pack of wheel and ratchet from the lead Wheeler - choreographer for the 24 • CINEFEX 22 claws, put that down and talk,\" explained Wilson. \"But it was difficult to coordinate, because there were different people on each foot and a separate person on either wing. It doesn't sound like a lot, but to actually coordinate it was something else.\" The task was finally accomplished by placing Dorothy in the foreground and using her body to block out all the puppeteers. \"About the most fun,\" Wilson continued, \"was having Billina trying to pull down a curtain cord in Mombi's palace, where Dorothy, Tik Tok and Billina are trapped. My hand was actually through the curtain behind her - I was going in from underneath while she was trying to pull the cord down with her beak. I had to sit in a crouched position on both legs, with dust and awful mayhem everywhere. My legs were going to sleep. My arms were going to sleep. It was quite interesting.\" Ultimately, the subtleties were established through long-suffering teamwork and cooperation. \"Rehearsal,\" Lyle Conway asserted, \"and lots of it. We videotaped one rehearsal where Bellina was singing 'My Way' - the Frank Sinatra recording. It really came together. Ad-libbing was difficult, however, because we were working with five people. But it came down to a point where if I said something, everybody managed within a beat or two to get the eyes in position, or the lids. It's strange - almost as if they were reading my mind, they were doing so well.\" One of the keys to the successful ensemble performance was the use of Mak Wilson's voice. \"I needed to do it when I was operating. We did one shot with Billina after we hadn't done one for a while, and I tried to do it without the voice. I couldn't. In the end, I had to use it; it was just a part of it. Once you get that chicken in your voice, you can't help it.\" Even when Billina takes refuge in the relative seclusion of Jack Pumpkinhead's hollow skull, it required three or four operators to bring her to life. \"Of course you can't see the whole chicken,\" Wilson said, \"so we just did the head. But even that took three people - one moved the bladder in the neck to give it a breathing movement, somebody else did the blinking, and I did the head myself. The eyes are the things that really give it its life. You can move it around, but if you haven't got anything there in the eyes - in the soul, as it were - it's useless. Dave Greenaway constructed Billina's eyes. He's done eyes all over the world - Yoda and everybody.\" In the completed version of Return to Oz, shots of the mechanical chicken are intercut with footage of a real Plymouth Rock hen. Billina's stand-in is used most often for longer shots where the bird is featured alone or where convenient angles for framing out simply don't exist. However, the barnyard fowl proved somewhat more temperamental than the heroine of the \"You can tame them,\" commented Mak Wilson, model shop. \"but you can't actually train them.\" The mechanical Billina was not only more cooperative than her stunt double, but delivered the essence of chicken-ness with equal panache. She was, in fact, so convincing, that outsiders simply couldn't tell the difference between the lifelike, automated version and the living bird. Rushes of the mechanical hen were sent to Burbank from England, and the studio executives to whom they were shown were certain that they were being toyed with - the fabricated Billina seemed so genuine that they were convinced the puppeteers had slipped in a real bird. While Billina and her friend Dorothy are as familiar to audiences as any residents of Kansas could be, the remainder of the cast of characters from L. Frank Baum's imagination are clearly from another world. And because the inventions of Tik Tok and Jack Pumpkinhead - Dorothy Gale's newest companions in Return to Oz- are grounded only in the reality of Norman Reynolds' designs, the model shop did not have to compete with Mother Nature in bringing convincing, earthbound creatures to In fact, Tik Tok - something of a Tin Man surrogate - is a mechanical man, and was built as a purely mechanical creature, operated by a full-sized man completely concealed within its spherical torso. \"We spent a lot of time on the design of Tik Tok,\" said Norman Reynolds. \"He took forever -there's an incredible amount of detail involved. And he has specific functions that had to be resolved - knocking the Wheelers, for instance, actually striking them with his lunch pail.\" It was executing the designs and making the functions both achievable and believable that proved to be most time consuming for the animatronics department. Transforming the drawings of the mechanical man into a walking, talking reality began with supervising plasterer Paul Jiggins and his crew. Jiggins - another Greystoke. veteran - first made a plaster template of the body shape, to serve as a mold, and later covered it with sheets of Kevlar. The lightweight substance was cut and sized - almost like cloth - to fit the template, in what was usually no more than a single, flexible layer. \"You have to cut a pattern, as if you were cutting carpet or clothes,\" said Lyle Conway. \"And you have to leave room for darts to create interlocking, inner pieces that you then press into shape.\" Even though the Kevlar is a difficult material to shape, its sturdiness and durability made it a worthwhile substance to use. \"It doesn't fold into contours the way fiberglass does,\" explained Tim Rose, who was responsible for the overall construction, \"but it's incredibly tough. Tik Tok took a number of face-forward dives during the filming; and if he'd had a fiberglass head, we'd have had to start from scratch. But with Kevlar, we just popped everything back out into place.\" The mechanical man's mechanics were not quite as simple, however. \"They tried originally, on one of the early prototypes,\" continued Rose, \"to make a full performer costume with everything completely working inside. It was an amazing looking contraption, but it was just a bit much for him to deal with. We went through funny stages. There were camera irises in his eyes that went in and out at one point, with dimming lights behind them. Finally we switched to the more simplified model you see The final rendition of Tik Tok was actually three different models and a number of additional parts - two 'Dirties,' as they"],["25","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 25"],["26","Return to Oz","26 • CINEFEX 22"],["27","Return to Oz","were called, dressed in various states of disrepair for the sequences where the mechanical man fights for Dorothy along their perilous journey; and one 'Bright 'n' Shiny,' used only in the coronation scene near the end of the film. \"They're not complete Tik Toks,\" Rose said. \"To do all the special effects, we've had to use bits and pieces - take 'em apart and put 'em back together. Cannibalize them.\" The performer who climbed inside the spherical torso to do the operating was Michael Sundin, an acrobat and a former member of the stage show Cats. The task required both stamina and courage. \"Michael's about five five,\" Conway commented, \"and Tik Tok's about four foot. So Michael had to bend with his head between his legs, bolted in, and he did the actual walkingaround in the thing - backwards. Sometimes, in the morning, he wouldn't be able to fit: so it was just a matter of forcing him in. Then everything loosened up, and his body settled into it. When you took the suit off of him, this rush of hot air hit you and there were pools of sweat at the bottom of the thing.\" At first Sundin was imprisoned within the mechanical man for no more than fifteen minutes, though by the end of shooting he had worked his way up to almost an hour. Because cramped quarters allowed Sundin little room to operate Tik Tok, external assistance was required to mobilize the character's arms - a task best achieved by creating only the illusion of arm movement. \"Since most of the scenes are done in short takes,\" explained Rose, \"we could just change the arm's armature at the elbow and put it in a different position for each shot. Then, when the whole thing was edited together, it appeared as if he was doing a lot more than he actually was.\" Sundin was able to help out with the manipulation of the arm by lifting his shoulder from inside Tik Tok - a movement that caused the whole mechanism to be pulled down slightly. External radio control facilitated movement in other areas - the eye blinks, the mustache and the head postion - via an eight-channel transmitter devised by Rose. \"We took the standard radio control you'd use for model airplanes, but then we modified the transmitters so it was better for flying a creature than it was an Ironically, the portions of the mechanical man that looked as if they should have helped him move were actually just window dressing - and annoying ones at that. The keys which supposedly power the patented mechanical man who does everything but live' were actually battery-powered, rigged with switches so that they could run down on cue, and the small chains that drape from shoulder to elbow to wrist were purely cosmetic. \"They don't actually help it in any way,\" said Rose, \"but we still wanted them for the look. The trimmings only helped to complicate things even more.\" Unlike Tik Tok, which was almost entirely a mechanical construct, Jack Pumpkinhead - the character made of sticks instead of straw, with a jack-o'-lantern for a head - was a manually controlled puppet. Although the creature was initially conceived solely as a marionette, the need for a more versatile Rear wheels were mounted on metal platforms with tennis shoes and leg reinforcements attached. Front wheels were affixed to foot-long extensions, with elbow supports, hand grips and brake mechanisms inside. The tops of the Wheeler helmets featured grotesque faces. / Pons Maar as the lead Wheeler. / Seventeen performers practiced for weeks to become proficient with the Wheeler mechanisms. / An early mockup to determine the ideal configuration for Mombi's Wheeler-powered sleigh. / Robert Scifo adds airbrush highlights to a matte painting of the ruined Emerald City as seen from the tower room of Mombi's palace. All of the film's matte paintings were executed at the Disney Studios in Burbank. CINEFEX 22 • 27"],["28","Return to Oz","To escape from the tower Gump - a strange amalgam of sofas, palm fronds and a mounted trophy head lashed together with twine and brought alive with Mombi's magical powder of life. In the animatronics shop, Nick Rayburn fits a portion of the Gump's foam rubber skin Gauging their actions on a closed-circuit video monitor, Stephenson, Lyle Conway cable-controlled moves. / Dorothy confers with the Gump and the Scarecrow. room, Dorothy and her friends assemble the over its mechanized fiberglass underskull. / Chris Eveleigh, John and Steve Norrington coordinate the Gump's Jack soon became apparent in order to accommodate diverse locations and unusual performing circumstances. The result was a collection of fiberglass sticks topped by a foam rubber head that was brought to life by master puppeteer Brian Henson, leading a team that worked cable-operated, fully articulated hands and a relatively immobile, but oddly expressive, pumpkin head. And in the event that it was simply impossible to use the puppet Jack, alternate plans were made to use a human stand-in. Jack Pumpkinhead was, in fact, two puppet versions of the same character - a full-sized Floppy Jack,' operated out of frame through the use of long poles, or from beneath the stage via rods; and a 'Trolley Jack,' for shots that were strictly above the waist. \"Trolley Jack was a more or less straightforward puppet,\" said Chris Ostwald, who was in charge of construction of all the mechanical Jacks. \"It was mounted on a four-wheeled trolley, so that he could appear to walk around the set. The whole body was controlled by one rod that gave all the forward and side-to-side action. As Brian turned his head, the body would follow and move round with the head. In addition, a special shock absorber and spring was mounted in the back, so that as the trolley was moved along, Jack would appear to actually be taking steps. There was even an adjustable stop, so they could be large steps or small steps.\" As Henson lay on his back in the custom-upholstered trolley, two other operators followed behind, operating a pair of levers that constituted the only controls for Jack's head and mouth. And while Floppy Jack's hand featured stick-like fingers that were actually stiff armatures molded and bent like wire, Trolley Jack was endowed with a full set of cable-operated hands constructed by Ostwald. \"The fingers were made out of wood, and were meant to look like carved fingers. We used airplane cable in most places, and nylon where the line threatened to cut into some of the wood. There were two fingers on one servo, a single finger on another one and the thumb on a separate one again. The wrist had one, as well. All this had to be housed inside a single shape - and in fact, I had to increase the size of it in places to get the big wheels of the servo inside. Radio-controlled arms were used when we wanted to have the hands of the puppet down by its sides. The shoulders were also strung so that the weight of the arms kept them level, and it was easy for the puppeteers to drop Jack's shoulders by pulling down, or to shrug his shoulders by lifting the weight off. The joints themselves were made from a tough rubber product used in manufacturing conveyer belts, while the wrists were a hard foam rubber.\" Floppy Jack, on the other hand, was used for the few shots in which all of the character's body was in frame - first when he is seen rising from the ground and standing up, and later as he lands and recovers after his fall from the back of the disintegrating Gump. \"The puppet was totally floppy,\" said Ostwald. \"It didn't support itself at all. And since it was made of fiberglass, it was too heavy to armature. So we designed its legs to lock off at the joints - to keep it from being totally limp -although it was not possible to actually support the puppet out level. A pole-arm rig manipulated the figure's grosser movements - one long rod connected to his back, and another to his pelvis - with radio control contributing the minimal movement of his The other major appearance by Floppy Jack featured the character falling to a snowy embankment, completely headless, followed moments later by his head which lands squarely on his pencil-thin neck - upside down. \"As he's flying,\" Ostwald explained, \"he's holding onto the back of the sofa as well as a broomstick. We had only two days to prepare for this, so we very quickly made pieces of polyfoam and threw them onto an armature of steel. This Jack was very light, and was actually supported by the broomstick hanging down and through the pelvis. It was this broomstick that was actually supporting the puppet, with the legs holding themselves up on the back seat. When he lands, he meets up with his head again about a second later. We did this with a cherry picker and a wire to his neck. A masked funnel inside the head helped keep it on course as the crew dropped the head down the wire, and it went shooooclunk! Right into place.\" The snow-covered set onto which he lands was one of the few elevated sets used on Oz, and it provided an opportunity to manipulate Floppy Jack from beneath, using rods that came up through the floor. \"We cut a hole in the floor,\" Ostwald continued, \"and sealed off the holes with spandex to stop all the snow from coming down on us. The legs were rodded from the hips, so that they could actually lift up and move around, while the elbows rested on the ground and were controlled from underneath.\" On the flying sofa, better known as the Gump - in of the few one of the few scenes involving mattes - Jack was worked directly by the puppeteers. Dressed in black, and shooting against a black background, the puppeteers operated the large figure by means of handles in the back and in the rear of the head. But whether Jack's head was upside down or right-side up, it was still the center of attention. The head is basically a foam rubber jack-o'-lantern, made to look completely hollow -at least hollow enough to hide a chicken for part of the adventure. \"The head was completely fabricated - not fitted to a core as you would an ordinary mask - in order to get the size right. It was first cast up as a solid piece - with mouth, nose and eyes modeled in - and then recast in a foam rubber mold that was welded from within to reinforce the structure. The movement of the head was manipulated by cables attached to plastic strips hinged together inside the face. By pulling the cable about twice as far as the operator wished the movement to be, two pulleys came closer together, making the mouth open. Ultimately, three Jack heads were created - one that looks good in closeup, one used primarily for stunts and one with a chicken inside.\" Another aspect of the head's ability to convey expression was in its actual angle. An adjustable gimbal was installed at the neck for tipping, and was linked by four wires with a bottlescrew adjustment on each that could accommodate the cable as"],["29","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 29"],["30","Return to Oz","30 • CINEFEX 22"],["31","Return to Oz","it was stretched. The control for the mechanism was particularly heavy-duty - a clutch-release mechanism from a Volkswagen Beetle. That simple mechanism was the full extent of Jack Pumpkinhead's facial articulation - no eye movement or radio control was used. Lyle Conway, however, had some reservations about the approach. \"You can intellectualize that if Jack came to life, this is the way he would be. But you sort of have to wonder if audiences aren't going to look at it and think we couldn't make the model's mouth move.\" While Floppy Jack was used in full-length shots where framing out was possible and Trolley Jack was used for closeups, a live performer - dressed in complete Jack ensemble - was used in shots where neither model would work. In costume, complete with polyfoam sticks extending from the ends of his arms and sporting a helmet inside one of the foam heads, there was only one problem - the actor's neck was about five times bigger than those of Floppy and Trolley. \"He's not as thin as he should be,\" allowed Conway, \"but the crew did go to great lengths to try and get the thinnest possible man available. John Stephenson was walking down the street and saw a six-foot-four fellow who weighed about 140 pounds. He was perfect - had absolutely no shape. You could put a hand around his thighs. But he couldn't move - he couldn't walk in character. We let him go and got a street mime who's really tall, but probably a lot heavier than he should be. His stage name is Fruit L'Orange - actually Stewart Larange. \" The original concept had been to use a human Jack no more than a third of the time; but as shooting progressed, the performer was relied upon quite heavily. \"Hopefully,\" said Conway, \"those shots will be short enough that you won't be able to concentrate on that fact.\" For Pons Maar, choreographing the movement of Jack Pumpkinhead was a top priority, and having a background in mime himself - not to mention dance, gymnastics and martial arts - developing a personality through movement was second nature to him. \"Jack was a whole new character. He's made of sticks, has a pumpkin for a head, is at least six-and-a-half - if not seven - feet tall. How does he move? What's his character like? How can his knees go up before his whole body crashes down - or do his knees bend at all? What does he do with his arms? How do his fingers move? What the heck is this guy? While I was still in San Francisco, I started to work a little with a friend of mine - Wayne Dolba, who is also a mime - on some basic movements for Jack. I got all the illustrations from the books that I could find, xeroxed them, put them up on the wall and just looked at them. I started thinking of animals, started thinking of long, thin people. Essentially, I thought of black basketball players who, because they are so long, move a certain way: but they also seem to have a rhythm - there's a little jump in their step. And they're relaxed. Very long-legged birds are intriguing. too. Then I started looking for cartoons, and got some ideas there. There was even a Disney cartoon character, Ichabod Crane, that had a real nice walk. \"Wayne and I spent hours trying to find a walk that seemed to work. He would get an idea and it would spark something in me; and I would try it, and that would spark something in him, and he would try it. We finally came up with what I felt was a real good basis for the movement of this character. Then I went over to England and was faced with this puppet -and not just one puppet - but several puppets.\" When Brian Henson was brought in to operate the puppet Jack characters, Pons Maar's task became even more complicated. \"At that point, I really just had to step back and let Brian take over and see what he could do - what was possible with the puppet. From that point, it was a matter of watching what he was doing, and then training Stewart to move like a puppet. Of course, at first I was opposed to that. I thought, 'You're a person, and a person's always capable of giving much more variety of movement.' Then I saw what Brian was doing, and - working within the limitations of that puppet - he brought it to life. He created this character that was, for me, much more alive than the human character. So I had to do an about face and say: 'Yeah, now I see. Now I understand.' From then on, it was a matter of finding out what scene was coming next and working first with Brian, then with Stewart, to match the movements and mannerisms that were evolving for Jack. I would ask Brian, 'What would Jack do here?' Brian would usually say, 'Well, I would imagine he would react like this.' And when I saw what he had in mind, I'd have some ideas, and then we'd take what we'd figured to Stewart and coach him on it. It was an ongoing process.\" For Pons Maar, the occupation of orchestrating movement for the residents of Oz was not limited to characters originating in the model shop. Prosthetics and other applications helped to enhance many of the decidedly nonhuman beings who were portrayed by very human performers. One whole group that relied almost entirely on the use of prosthetics was the bizarre and frantic Wheelers - the strange inhabitants who terrorize Dorothy upon her arrival in the ruins of the Emerald City. \"The Wheelers were originally in the Baum books,\" said Maar, who had been deeply involved in the evolution of the creatures and their movement from the very beginning, \"and Walter definitely wanted the Wheelers in. So they set about designing them when it still wasn't certain whether it was possible for people to do the rolling around.\" \"In the beginning,\" said John Stephenson, who built the apparatus that enabled the Wheelers to function as such, \"nobody knew exactly how we were going to achieve this. We talked of motorized versions and all sorts of things, but I always thought the best thing we could do was actually get guys to do it.\" Keeping this in mind, Stephenson went to work developing riding gear that could be manpowered by the seventeen operators - and be constructed by the requisite time-saving, costreducing means of mass production. What Stephenson and crew eventually created was a set of prosthetics - wheeled leg extensions attached to tennis shoes for the back, and foot-long extensions with wheels and brakes in the front - all covered by the gaudy costumes characteristic of the Wheelers. The track-mounted Gump is prepared for its initial takeoff. / Dorothy whispers words of encouragement to the just-enlivened creature as Mombi storms into the tower room to thwart their escape. / With Dorothy, Tik Tok and Billina sitting on the commandeered sofas and Jack Pumpkinhead - the 'Floppy Jack' version - clinging on from behind, the Gump crashes through the balcony of the tower room and sails out over the Emerald City. In reality, the breakaway set was built only a few feet off the ground and the Gump was mounted safely on rails. CINEFEX 22 • 31"],["32","Return to Oz","Background plates for the flying sequence were derived from a variety of sources. The forest surrounding the Emerald City, for example, was fashioned in miniature others employed location photography. / A sprawling, forced-perspective miniature featuring the ruined Emerald City and the Nome King's Robert Spencer renders the foreground Deadly Desert in Perisic, director of model and background plate employed during the Gump's passage over Oz. / With puppeteers huddled out of camera range, the Gump is readied for one of its flying shots. / Perisic's Zoptic system - a modified which creates the illusion of movement in depth within a for certain shots, while mountainous lair. Artist painting form. / Zoran process photography, examines a VistaVision front projection system background plate - was used for the Gump's flying scenes. 32 - CINEFEX 22 \"In back was a metal platform the tennis shoe fit on,\" explained Maar - who, in addition to his other taskings was also cast as the lead Wheeler. \"Coming up on either side of the platform were bars that were reinforced across the front, so you just slid your foot into the shoe, strapped it around just above the shin and below the knee, and you were in. And of course, we wore knee pads, because the only way we could let ourselves down - easily, that is - was by going down on our knees. The arm mechanism was similar. It had an elbow support and a handgrip inside, with a pull for a brake.\" \"The wheels were from Holland,\" continued Stephenson, \"and apparently used in the manufacture of wheelchairs. We actually looked at lots of different sorts of wheels, and the simplicity of these was right. They also had solid hubs, so we were able to drill out and put bearings in either side, and then put on plastic hubcaps, so the trouser covered them and all you could see was a spinning thing. It wasn't obvious whether there were forks or brakes or anything. Stainless steel parts held the wheels in place with four bolts, so if anything went wrong, you could just slot in another unit. The trick of the whole thing was a special ratchet that allowed the wheel to spin one way, but not the other. That meant the performers could stand on the wheels without sliding backwards -which enabled them to push off sideways, much in the way an ice skater pushes a skate.\" \"It still surprises me that we could do it,\" said Maar. \"But once the research had started - the designing, the making of the actual apparati, the getting of people to try - then they needed a character for the Wheelers. And that's where I came into the picture. Walter knew about the kind of nutty, psychotic, extreme portrayals that I do on stage, and he said, 'That's exactly what I was thinking of for the Wheelers!'\" The nutty, extreme portrayals evolved from Maar's experience as a performing mime. \"As a mime, you put the whiteface on, and you have to move a certain way and tell stories and do business - but I didn't like working the street. I wanted something a little more controlled, indoors, so people would come and see me as opposed to my trying to grab them while they're going someplace else and really have no interest - when I'm just another of that army of mimes they're sick of. So I toured with a punk band for a year - doing sax, drums and vocals -and then toured with a theatre group. But I finally got tired of trying to work within groups and started doing solo work. It was during a performance of my solo show in Bolinas, near San Francisco, that I ran into Walter Murch - and here I am.\" Drawing from the rich reserves of his imagination, as well as his unusual background, Maar put a lot of effort into developing the role of the Wheelers. \"They're really loud and fierce looking animals that have no feet, no claws. In fact, physically, they have no way of hurting you other than to band together, wear bright, gaudy clothes, scream as loud as they can and act as menacing as possible. But if their bluff is called, they get out of there - they hit the trail. They're also Mombi's guard dogs. They keep people away from what's left of the Emerald City. And they're"],["33","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 33"],["34","Return to Oz","34 - CINEFEX 22"],["35","Return to Oz","The Wheelers' slightly ridiculous ferocity also extended to their wardrobe. \"The costumes are all one piece,\" continued Maar, \"similar to a big leotard. The legs were flared to cover their feet, and their pants were very bright colors, as were the vests and the long coats with tails. The coats were made from a leather fabric in different kinds of patches - all very similar, but all different, too. And when they first appear, they have on helmets with faces on the tops of their heads - so that when they come straight on, it looks like there are all these horrible faces coming at you.\" Once the characters were fully designed, Maar and others had to find people to actually attempt the rather bizarre acrobatics required for the roles. \"We originally started by hiring ice skaters and roller skaters,\" John Stephenson recalled, \"thinking that that would be the sort of people to do it. But in actual fact, it wasn't. None of the people who ended up as the Wheelers were either ice or roller skaters.\" Pons Maar agreed: \"Nothing led into being able to do it. Being able to high dive, gymnastics - nothing transferred over to make it easy. What we found when we first started was that no muscles in our body were ready for this - it was such an alien configuration.\" Stunt arranger Colin Skeaping helped set up a physical workout regimen for the Wheeler finalists - a total of seventeen performers, including some former 'apes' from Greystoke and a man whose feet had been amputated years before and therefore required a specially modified set of wheel extensions. \"In the beginning,\" said Stephenson, \"they put in about one or two hours, because they found that it required new muscles in the legs, especially in the groin area. By shooting time, however, most of the Wheelers could do two or three hours at a time. They used to do laps at the studio - just go round and round. Body position was always important. We kept trying to stress to the guys to keep their backs straight and keep it on all fours. There was a tendency to get the front wheels as far forward as they could, and the back wheels forward, too - and the result was a not very nice shape. A lot of the training was just getting them to learn how to stand on all fours, like a four-legged animal does. But once they learned where their centers of gravity were, they could always bring their body backwards or forwards to relieve the strain on their muscles.\" \"In developing the characterizations for the Wheelers,\" Maar continued, \"we really tried to develop specific personalities. We had one who was real dumb, and we had others who were more frightening. There was a group of us that were progressing along similar lines, and we would stick together and play. That's when we really found things. Once we were all together, we weren't constantly thinking, 'I wonder if I can do this?' We were just playing. We'd call each other names, we'd ridicule. We'd act like ignorant animals, having fun - and the unconscious, unstructured character work was much easier then.\" One of the most challenging sequences involved having six of the Wheelers actually pulling a sleigh driven by the witch Mombi. The scene was shot in two different ways - one in which the Wheelers physically dragged the fiberglass chariot, and another in which long bars, hidden from the camera, extended from the rig and supported the Wheelers'bodies. \"They laid over the bar,\" explained Stephenson, \"which meant they could lift their arms and legs off the ground in order to simulate wheeling.\" Footage from both versions - some shots of them pulling the sleigh from the front, as well as angles of the performers utilizing the support for a really fast, over-the-shoulder sideshot - was finally used. Even the accompanying scene, in which the Wheelers travel at immense speed into a dark tunnel, ending in a pile-up at the far side, did not require fast-motion or enhancement. \"The guys got so good at it, that they could actually travel at quite a lot of speed. Once they got going, especially on a flat floor — they went really fast. I mean, really!\" John Stephenson worked on the Wheelers for over a year - designing them, then redesigning change after change until the incredible project was finished. \"At the beginning, we were thinking of all sorts of weird and wonderful ways of doing it - trolleys they would lay over, motorized things. And we were getting far too complicated. What we needed were just those little ratchets, and that wheel really does it all now.\" While the Wheelers may indeed be weird and wonderful, that description does not even begin to set forth an image of the creature that is perhaps the oddest inhabitant in the Land of Oz - a living thing with the head of a green, horned camel, a body made of two sofas tied together with twine and functional wings made of palm fronds - in other words, the Gump. The character itself is only barely a Baum creation, with the crew of Return to Oz taking enormous liberties - and having a good deal of fun - with what began as a rather obscure reference in one of the Oz sequels. \"There is one illustration in a book somewhere,\" Norman Reynolds recalled, \"a very strange-looking mouselike thing with a wispy mane. But it's nothing like our Gump. Ours is totally fabricated.\" Created by Dorothy and her friends as part of a desperate escape from Mombi's palace, the Gump is one of the most charming and delightful characters in the film - and also ushered in one of the toughest sequences of When the Gump was at rest, shooting it was relatively easy. The cables - which were rigged using a Nick Reyburn design - extended more than twenty feet away from the construct so that various operators could work the head, wings and other appendages from various hiding places out of frame. And framing out the operators posed no problems with such a large critter. The real challenge in the film came when virtually all of the major characters - short of Mombi and the Nome King - climb onto the back of this marvelous creature and goad it into crashing through a stone balcony. The Gump and its passengers fly low and wobbly over the ruins of the Emerald City, across the forest and the Deadly Desert to the foothills of the Nome King's mountain - where the thin twine that held the creature intact gives way and it disassembles in mid-air, landing in pieces on a Nicol Williamson as the Nome King - a mad monarch made of stone who metamorphoses into nearhuman form as his power over others grows. Makeup supervisor Robin Grantham designed a two-stage makeup for Williamson - one of which looked very much like a predominantly rock-based figure. / Williamson undergoes the obligatory life-casting process in preparation for his role. / Grantham and chief hairdresser Patricia McDermott add finishing touches to the Nome King in his alternate quasi-human CINEFEX 22 • 35"],["36","Return to Oz","Additional versions of the Nome King were brought to life via clay animation in the discusses storyboards for the sequence with Walter which first spots Dorothy McKean animates a closeup of the Nome King's hand. / A Nome King's visage appears release of her friends and restoration of the Emerald City. / Douglas Aberle gauges a move on the miniature Jack puppet, which is about to become a snack for the studios of Will Vinton Productions. Vinton Murch. / Craig Bartlett animates the Nome spy and her friends on the mountain ledge. / Bruce mere suggestion of the on the rocky cliffside as Dorothy pleads for the final-stage Nome King. 36 4 CINEFEX 22 rocky snow-covered ledge below. To achieve the flying effect, Zoran Perisic was consulted as to the possible use of a miniature Gump. \"I was never really very keen on that. For one thing, we were dealing with puppets already, and to make miniatures of the puppets move realistically didn't make any sense. Instead, for some of the very long shots, I filmed situations where we used the Gump as a silhouette flying across the moon, which we would then composite across an ordinary background of moon \"For the closeups and everything, the operators were lying all around the couches - underneath the couches - with all these mechanisms to make the wings move and to support and operate the puppets. It was quite a thing in itself, just trying to hide these people. The Gump head was operated by long cables, with a second version operated by radio control when the head had nothing behind it to frame out the cables - as in the scene where it comes apart and falls. Its eyes are open and its mouth opens, and there's nothing behind. That had to be radioStill, for the longer sequences, the necessary close-in shots of the creature and its occupants required that a choice be made - bluescreen or front projection. For Zoran Perisic, the choice seemed obvious. The optical effects expert was intimately familiar with the merits of front projection - specifically, Perisic's own patented Zoptic system. \"There were some people really against the idea of front projection - mainly because they had already bought the bluescreen, and it seemed more economic for them to go with it. Obviously, I'm biased. I use bluescreen just like everyone else - there are very good reasons for using bluescreen sometimes - but you have to have pretty good reasons. Photographically, it just never, never can match front projection.\" Although a more traditional bluescreen system was rigged - and tested - the method provided less than satisfactory results. The major problem arose while attempting to produce a convincing flying effect for the Gump - made particularly difficult by the requisite speed at which the Gump's palm-frond wings moved in order to create the illusion of flight. \"The main requirement for bluescreen work,\" explained Perisic, \"is that you have sharp images, because you have to pull off a very sharp traveling matte. But we did a test, and at the speed Walter liked, the wings streaked - there was a smudge as they moved through the frame. When we cut down on the shutter to get sharper images, it strobed. Also, because the flight takes place at dusk with moonlight, any kind of subtle lighting was badly affected by this enormous blue light source which was the blue screen \"Originally, somebody else's company was going to do the front projection, but the main difference was that nobody else had the Zoptic system. The Zoptic system is different from ordinary front projection. Simple front projection begins with linear movement of an object across a background. For example, you can make a seagoing ship move across the frame; but"],["37","Return to Oz","CINEFEX 22 • 37"],["38","Return to Oz","38 • CINEFEX 22"],["39","Return to Oz","instead of putting it on wheels and pushing it from one end of the frame to the other, you can have a flimsy thing that is perfectly static. All you have to do is make sure the screen behind it is large enough, and move the camera instead of the ship. The projector and the camera are linked together, so the background projected onto the screen is copied by the camera directly. As you pan along with the camera, the ship will look as if it is actually sailing across the screen in the opposite direction; and if you want it to ride the waves, you do a bit of tilting at the same time. \"Consider an airplane. You can make it bounce up and down against the background of clouds and sky. If you could make a rig that would actually rotate around the lens axis, it would look as if that airplane was spinning as it was flying. You can do all that with a sophisticated camera/projector system - but what you cannot do is make the thing move in depth. That is really where the Zoptic principle starts. By using a zoom lens and projecting a large picture on the background, and by actually mounting the foreground item on a fully articulated pole thrust through the front projection screen, the Zoptic system allows the subject to appear closer or farther from the camera while the background itself remains relatively the same. You can zoom in, zoom out, pan, tilt, yaw -anything that would enhance the shot - and the background does not look as if it has changed at all. I've developed a special rig that can pan, tilt and rotate 360 degrees and give absolute fluidity of movement. But the key to the Zoptic idea is this - everything that actually looks as if it's moving in the frame is not, and everything that looks as though it's static is really moving.\" A sophisticated array of pole-arms, motion control rigs and linked camera-projector arrangements worked visual miracles when the Zoptic system was employed in the Superman films. Now Zoran Perisic's innovation would be applied in a different, but no less effective way in Oz. To collect the necessary aerial footage of clouds, sky, forest and desert, a team was dispatched to Algeria and Sardinia, among other locations. In the final film, the Gump and its passengers would begin their journey over the ruins of the Emerald City, fly over the forest and then continue across the Deadly Desert. But even though the entire episode was scheduled to take place during a moonlit night, none of the plates were photographed 'day for night.' All footage was shot in straight daylight. \"I didn't want to stop down until the final compositing,\" Perisic explained. \"I felt this would give better detail. Process stuff you shoot clean, with no filtration, so that it's as clean and sharp an image as you can get. Then you can tinker with it as much as you like when rephotographing. Sometimes, however, it's a bit disturbing for people to look at that. And it can depend on how strong the special effects man is - either you try and shoot it to please people who are looking at the dailies, or you try and shoot to please people when they see the final film. It's easy to degrade an image, to blend it in and fog it out. But you can do all that at the last stage; you don't have to start beautifying it at the background stage.\" To further enhance the quality, the plates were shot in VistaVision, with final compositing on projection format 35mm. Meanwhile, two separate rigs for photographing the full-size Gump were being arranged. \"One was a marvelous hydraulicoperated rig that was supported from underneath,\" said Perisic, \"which meant you could never see the underside of the Gump. All the down shots and three-quarter shots looking over things used this rig. It would pitch and roll on command - very useful. The other rig was a very, very large pole-arm that had another set of couches on it. You couldn't do very much with it during the shot, but you could change the angle at which it was set for any specific shot. I wanted to be able to see under the Gump for all the long shots. So with careful planning on the pole-arm, it was possible to do all the shots looking up, looking threequarter forward and three-quarter back, and getting the motion of the wing flapping - going up and down and so on. It worked beautifully - you can't tell the difference between one support system and the other.\" To complete the effect of the Gump and its companions soaring over Oz and environs, a number of miniatures were built - using Norman Reynolds designs and constructed under the supervision of art director Charles Bishop. Most were used as background for the flying scenes. \"We basically had three sets in miniature,\" said Gary Kurtz. \"One was strictly to allow the flying sequences to work. We needed the background for them flying away; and that background was a miniature of the ruined city. We had a few other miniatures that were part of the devastation of the Nome kingdom, which we had to build about a third of normal size to get the destruction of the set to work right on The large-scale miniatures included a twenty-foot in diameter ornament room set that was originally used in shots to establish scale: and was later shown collapsing - through the special rigging of mechanical effects supervisor lan Wingrove and crew. The Nome mountain miniature was another model destined to be blown up - this time on a studio backlot. Filmed with a real sky for a backdrop, the ten-foot-high construct was built in breakaway fashion so that the explosion which ultimately destroyed it would do so along predetermined lines. Mombi's tower - from which the Gump makes its daring escape - was also created in miniature. \"The tower is supposed to be about 280 feet tall in the finished film,\" said Reynolds, \"but it was actually fifteen feet tall on stage. The ruins of the Emerald City are quite large as well, something on the order of fifty feet by sixty feet — a considerable size.\" Maintaining proper scale is always of great importance when filming miniatures, but shooting the weaving flight of the Gump -and making that flight convincing -posed a few unique challenges for the effects crew. \"We made up a special rig that flew across the miniature just to shoot the background plates,\" explained Perisic. \"It had a special periscope arrangement in order for the camera to get only about half-an-inch off Three versions of the shapeshifting Nome King were employed in the throne room scenes. Mark Gustafson animates the most human-looking of the three. The next metamorphic step would involve Nicol Williamson in the rockier of his two makeups. / The craggy, first-stage throne room figure. / Live-action reference footage of Williamson was employed by the animators to help instill their clay figures with recognizable nuances of character that would help audiences to mentally accept the transition between the clay-animated figures and the live performer in makeup. Bill Fiesterman consults the footage between moves. CINEFEX 22 • 39"],["40","Return to Oz","Before being wished back to Kansas, Dorothy and her friends - both old and new - unite with residents of the restored Emerald City for a grand celebration marking the coronation of Oz' rightful ruler, the Princess Ozma, long-ago enchanted by Mombi and imprisoned in a mirror. 40 • CINEFEX 22 the floor. And given the scale of the model, and the height at which the Gump was supposed to be flying - just barely clearing the obstacles - you had to get really low down.\" The final result was a dizzying flight that began with a lurching launch off of Mombi's tower (a miniature) continued in a low, unsteady swoop across the ruins of Oz (another miniature) and into the forest beyond - first a miniature and then real forest as dialogue continues. When morning comes, the disintegrating Gump finds itself on the Deadly Desert - in a shot utilizing real landscapes - and ends with a glimpse of the awesome Nome mountain - a miniature that, some time later, would explode. Finally, to assure the quality of the projection work, Perisic supervised both the photography and the timing. His greatest concern was maintaining the delicate relationship of light and dark that would create the illusion of night over Oz - a night filled with stars that were just a bit brighter than earthly stars. \"It's really the relationship between the stop you're using and the amount of spill light you have on the screen. You can tolerate a considerable amount of ambient light because the film has a latitude that is limited - it's much shorter, much narrower than the latitude of the eye in terms of contrast. Obviously, the main consideration is not to use low-contrast stock when making the plates; but the other thing is to balance the brightness of the stars with the brightness of the foreground, and to maintain that balance from shot to shot to shot so that when it's cut together afterwards, it doesn't look odd. Timing is really crucial. If you're a little bit up or down in the final grading, the stars can disappear or suddenly appear like golf balls. I made sure all the stars were shot under the same exposure, and all the printing was done in the same light -I actually went to have all the plates timed at the lab, and checked them. I also had a gray scale at the beginning of the plate, just to balance the foreground to the gray scale. Once you have that as a constant, the stars take care of themselves - even though when you actually looked through the camera at the stars, it was quite disturbing. They didn't look at all like they were actually photographed.\" Return to Oz probably couldn't be called a labor of love - the project was ultimately too complex, time-consuming and exhausting to enjoy such a description. But if the value of a film can be measured in the dedication of its director, and the faith a crew and other professionals put in that director, then Walter Murch has made himself a classic. There is, after all, that persistent and rather lovely story about Walter's friends - on the film and elsewhere -who rallied around him when he needed them most. For Walter Murch, that time of need came after two years of preparation and seven weeks of shooting, when the Return to Oz project simply reached overwhelming proportions. \"Walter's energies just ran out,\" Paul Maslansky recalled. \"But a picture like this has a momentum. You have five hundred people on payroll, and you don't just stop dead. If you do, it's tough to get started again. We ran through about 40,000 pounds a day - that's about $60,000. Walter needed some help. We considered replacing him, very seriously considered it. This is serious business. Sure, friendship enters into it; but you're dealing with $25 million, and a company like Disney which needs a hit. This is the major leagues, the big boys. It's not: 'Hey let's go down to the corner drugstore and put on a show!' Walter himself felt that his energies had been depleted, and with well over fifty percent of the schedule still to come, the tensions and pressures were \"About this time, George Lucas was in Japan. He heard about the situation, called up Dick Berger and volunteered to drop his business in Japan and come over immediately to backstop Walter - to get him back on his feet, redirect his energy. Maybe Walter could relax for a couple of days with him and talk over some things, and then come back on the set - with Walter still directing uniquely, but with George behind him. I would have been a fool to turn something like that down. I visited every corner of the lot, every little shop. I brought the floor crew together and I explained exactly what the situation was, so nobody was filled with rumor. It was very moving, the amount of support that was demonstrated for Walter by all of them - and about three days after George was here and we were all feeling very good, I got a call from my receptionist downstairs. She said, 'There's a Francis Ford Coppola to see you,' and I said, 'Send him up.' Francis said: 'I hear Walter's got some problems. I'm here to help. Tell me what you want me to do.' I said, 'Why don't you just go over there and put your arm around him?' The next thing I knew, Phil Kaufman turned up, on his way to Paris for the opening of The Right Stuff.\" \"I know there was one press report,\" said Gary Kurtz, \"that claimed George was hired as some kind of consultant on the film. It's just not true. Walter worked on THX 1138- George's first film -and on American Graffiti. And he's worked for Francis a lot, too. He also worked on The Right Stuff for Phil. And they were all just interested in seeing what he was doing. Like most directors, they didn't mind saying: 'Well, keep working and pay no attention to the studio. I like your material. Keep \"Walter deserves that kind of attention,\" Maslansky concluded. \"In the past, he has helped all of them tremendously. With credit or without it, it doesn't really matter, but Walter is a genius - like Francis and George. And there are very few of those people around.\" Walter Murch is indeed a person of unique talent and vision, and Return to Oz is a reflection of that vision. This first-time director with the long list of credits may very well have made a Disney film that both children and adults can enjoy - a movie about hope and friendship and courage - even though it features men who roll around on all fours and a talking chicken from the Midwest. In short, Walter Murch may have succeeded in presenting a story about an extraordinary, yet familiar place told in a brand new way - a story that's charming, delightful... and tough."],["41","Return to Oz",""],["42","Baby","BRINGING UP BABY"],["43","Baby","It was a difficult birth - one characterized by intense labor pains and unexpected complications. But then again, this was no ordinary infant. The offspring was a baby brontosaurus - the complex mechanical star born to Walt Disney's Touchstone Films in their fantasy adventure, Baby... Secret of the Lost Legend. After an arduous three-year gestation period - resulting in a film hailed by its producers as the greatest adventure ever born - Baby proved truly to be a reflection of its diverse and complex heritage. And although movies about dinosaurs are nothing new, this particular tale features a brontosaurus that was not only given the title role, but a sympathetic role playing opposite human actors in what is, at its very essence, a Baby's genealogical chart can be traced to its origins, when Clifford and Ellen Green decided to write a screenplay about the most basic kind of love - the love between a mother and her child. At the same time, their lifelong fascination with hidden animals and their dog's brontosaurus-shaped squeak toy inspired an unusual twist - resulting in Baby, the contemporary story of a young American couple (William Katt and Sean Young) who discover a family of dinosaurs living in a remote part of the African rain forest. Matters are complicated when a rival paleozoologist (Patrick McGoohan) arrives on the scene and claims the discovery as his own, killing the father and capturing the mother in the process. Baby is adopted by the couple, and the adventure is off and running as they try to reunite the infant with its mother. The Greens first presented their concept to producer Jonathan T. Taplin and executive producer Roger Spottiswoode, who - as producer and director, respectively - had just completed the politically-charged suspense drama, Under Fire. Taplin and Spottiswoode liked the concept and, deeming it a cross between Dumbo and Raiders of the Lost Ark, thought Walt Disney Productions would be an ideal place to take it. Disney vice president in charge of production Tom Wilhite agreed and immediately started the ball rolling by placing the project into development. Two months later, in the fall of 1982, a first draft script was ready and Baby shifted over into the early stages of formal preproduction. While Taplin turned his attentions to selecting both a director and an exotic locale for the film's location shooting, Wilhite focused on the equally vital task of searching for someone to design, engineer and manufacture the dinosaur 'stars.' He began by calling upon Disney's resident effects experts Harrison Ellenshaw and Peter Anderson for assistance in determining how the Baby photographs copyright ® 1985 by Touchstone Films. All rights reserved. Production unit still photography by Graham Attwood. Additional photography by Nancy Moran and John Scheele. Research and interview materials for portions of this article were provided by Randy and Jean-Marc Lofficier. Special thanks to Steve Rogers and Louise Spencer. baby brontosaurus might best be created, as well as in projecting its estimated cost. Others involved in the early discussion included Lee Dyer, who had just finished supervising the effects for Something Wicked This Way Comes; Ron Tantin, manager of Disney's special effects and prop shop; and Isidoro Raponi, a former associate of Carlo Rambaldi who had joined the Disney organization in 1979. One of the major difficulties in bringing a brontosaurus to life turned out to be its horizontal stance. Most film fantasy creatures tend to be vertical or upright, therefore lending themselves somewhat readily to the man-in-a-suit approach. The quadruped brontosaurus, obviously, did not. But with the amount of dinosaur screen time required for Baby, plus the degree of interaction with its human costars, creating a creature which relied upon opticals for insertion into the film would have been an expensive and logistically unwieldy proposition. The use of Disney's audioanimatronics was also considered, but ultimately rejected as too time-consuming to conform with the rigid ten-week location shooting schedule. And traditional stop-motion effects were simply out of the question. \"It would have taken several years to do this movie with stop-motion,\" commented Raponi. \"That technique is more for long shots without too much action. Here the creatures were more like actors - not just special effects. And time was a major factor.\" So, after much debate, it was decided to go ahead and have Raponi and Tantin devise a mechanically-controlled dinosaur suit, with human operators hunched inside to provide equilibrium and foot movements. As yet, no director had been assigned to the project. Accordingly, no storyboards were available from which to begin work in other areas. Thus, all energies during the early preproduction phase were focused on building a workable Baby. The first step was to create the dinosaur in miniature. James Kagel - who had previously worked on The Howling, Popeye and Altered States - was called upon to do the actual sculpting, while Raponi and Tantin began experimenting with various mechanisms. Working with oil-based clay, Kagel finished the first of two miniature baby dinosaurs in about four weeks. The sculpture - three feet long and nine inches in height - bore a remarkable resemblance to an iguana. This initial rendering was not quite what Taplin, Wilhite and the Greens had in mind, and so lifelong dinosaur aficionado John Scheele - an expert in motion control who had worked on Tron-was brought in for consultation. Scheele and his father, William - a renowned author of dinosaur books and a thirty-year director of the Cleveland Museum of Natural History - objected to the evil beady-eyed design, and together set about gathering accurate research to realize the majesty and beauty of the brontosaurus in a factual way. William Scheele began by preparing drawings and a sculptured head which was destined to become a benchmark for the ultimate dinosaur visage. Later on, he brought in his own personal sample of mummified hadrosaurus skin - the only existing Howard E. Green clue as to the actual appearance of dinosaur skin - and that"],["44","Baby","On location in Africa, Baby director B.W.L. Norton frames a closeup on his prehistoric star. / After an unsuccessful first attempt to produce a consultant William Scheele brontosaurus hatchling. In produced not only a number artistic liberties were taken with his studied concepts, including the repositioning from their actual forehead position to a more familiar and acceptable spot on the snout. / James Kagel was responsible for most of the dinosaur sculpting on the various alternatives, it was brontosaurus family called for in the script would be worn by human performers. convincing miniature prototype, paleontology was engaged to assist dinosaur creator Isidoro Raponi in developing an authentic-looking the process, Scheele of drawings, but also a sculpted head and neck. Though useful, certain of the creature's nostrils film. / After considering decided that the achieved in the form of articulated rubber suits 44 • CINEFEX 22 became the model for Baby's outer texture. Scheele also convinced the dinosaur designers that the tail actually served as a counterbalance and did not drag on the ground as commonly believed. John Scheele - who had earlier explored with stop-motion expert Jim Danforth the idea of using a motion control system for mobilizing Baby - agreed that although the mechanical approach was a necessary evil, the concept was basically sound. In all, he spent three months on the project; but as things became more politically charged and compromises were made, he reluctantly left to work on The Adventures of Buckaroo Banzai, returning only periodically for consultation. Special effects advisor Lee Dyer also parted Baby's company around this time, shortly after the spring 1983 hiring of director B.W.L. Although Baby was only Norton's third directorial challenge - following the cult classic Cisco Pike and More American Graffiti - Jonathan Taplin had been eager to work with him for some time. Both Howard Kazanjian and George Lucas had called personally to recommend him for the job; and for his part, Norton was anxious to try his hand at an effects film. The director's arrival put an end to Baby's preproduction malaise, marked by a level of indecision which had already lead to much wasted effort on the part of Isidoro Raponi and crew. The creature creators had been limping along with minimal information and guidance as to what the dinosaurs should look like and what would really be expected of them in the way of performance. It was a time for experimention - but no practical designs could be implemented without a director. Stepping in with authority, Norton helped to solidify the production's scattered efforts and at the same time gave its somewhat nebulous structure his own particular focus. Just prior to Norton's arrival, Kagel and Raponi had completed a second miniature sculpture, reflecting the input of John and William Scheele. The revised Baby was cuter and more juvenile in its facial features, and appeared a lot less reptilian. Certain artistic liberties were taken, such as moving the nostrils from the authentic forehead location to a more familiar below-theeyes position as it was decided that any sniffing activity would have looked silly had they been retained in the former site. Keeping these basic ideas in mind, a full-size dinosaur hatchling, approximately sixteen feet long and five feet high, was created using five tons of WED clay - a special Disney-designed water clay used for sculpting the EPCOT figures. Even though the clay was especially soft and easy to work with, the task still took eight weeks to complete. When it was finished, Norton made one of his first mandates as director - the Baby figure was too large. Raponi and his crew spent the next eight weeks scaling it \"The scale of Baby had to be reduced so that it didn't overpower our two human actors,\" explained Taplin. \"In a sense, we had to have an animal that could get into a tent between Bill and Sean, and be huge and kind of awkward - but not so big"],["45","Baby","CINEFEX 22 • 45"],["46","Baby","46 • CINEFEX 22"],["47","Baby","that it would crush them or make it impossible to see the actors. Baby still had to be lovable - like a big, big dog or something. The original version just seemed wrong. I think Norton's decision to make it smaller was totally right.\" Baby's size actually fluctuated quite often during preproduction, making life difficult for Raponi and crew. Initially, five-foot ten-inch dinosaur technician Philip Bartko served as the model for 'the man in the grey rubber suit' and a skeletal aluminum structure was built around him. When Baby was scaled down, a midget was brought in for a test casting. But Raponi found that little people were structurally incapable of supporting the fiftyplus pounds that the costume would weigh, and therefore decided that someone between four-feet nine-inches and five feet in height would provide the optimum size combined with the requisite strength. So the costume design was once again modified and the legs lengthened to accommodate a taller person. Another consideration in the quest for Baby's proper size was the reality that a full-size dinosaur would have been somewhere between sixty and seventy feet long, Keeping this in mind, plus the fact that Baby was described in the original script as being 'about the size of a Saint Bernard, helped to influence the final Although originally there were no plans to build a full-size adult dinosaur - only neck and tail portions that could be cleverly shot to create the illusion of an entire beast - Norton decided early on that a complete adult brontosaurus would be needed for establishing shots. Jim Kagel, along with five additional sculptors and another dozen plaster and metal workers, set to work constructing a seventy foot 'Big Mama.' Six weeks and forty tons of WED clay later, a sculpted facsimile of the brontosaurus matriarch stood in the Disney staff shop. Underneath its clay exterior was a network of five-inch-thick steel pipe, crosshatched rods and chicken wire covered with plaster and fiber. In the end, the armature turned out to be too small. As a result, instead of the proposed one-inch layer of clay, some sections required nearly two feet in thickness to achieve the desired shape. The completed sculpture was finally encased in a patchwork of forty plaster molds - each covering two-and-ahalf square feet - while the legs were unbolted and molded separately. From these molds, fiberglass castings were made and later shipped to Africa for final assembly. In addition to the full-size model, Kagel and his assistant, Pat Newman, sculpted a separate twelve-foot neck for the mother, a seventeen-foot neck for the father and a unisex twenty-one-foot tail. Ron Tantin then completed the task by devising a system of hydraulic lifts and cranes to mechanize these appendages. \"Sculpting the father's neck and tail,\" explained Kagel, \"was like sculpting a giant tube. The outer texture was changed three times. We finally ended up with something resembling elephant skin - gnarly, lumpy and with a lot of cracks and scales. We scratched folds and wrinkles in random patterns: and finally, in the end, just stood back and threw pieces of clay at it to create warts. For the full-size mother's body, we added the skin texture with a special paint roller that had been imprinted with a skin pattern.\" The full-size creatures were equipped with movable necks and tails, but it was determined that any additional maneuvers would require the use of miniatures. One-fourth scale models of both the mother and father dinosaur were needed as foreground or background elements in the composite shots - the scale being dictated by its ideal proportion to human operators. As he had with Baby, Kagel first sculpted a small clay model before tackling the fifteen-foot miniature from which the dinosaur suits would be made. While work on the adult dinosaurs was progressing - with Raponi busily supervising their construction and means of mechanization - an appropriate size for Baby had yet to be decided upon. After much consideration, Norton and production designer Raymond G. Storey determined that the baby brontosaurus would measure ten feet in length, with a mid-back height of forty inches. Raponi and crew were, at long last, authorized to initiate the process of molding and casting a test model of the film's star, as well as begin the search for suitable Baby operators. Another milestone that expedited the heretofore leisurely production pace was the hiring of veteran storyboard illustrator John Jensen. \"When I came in,\" said Norton, \"I found that they had storyboarded scenes that weren't even supposed to be in the movie. Also these storyboards were made by animators, so they weren't done in the proper perspective. We could never have done a lot of these shots because the proportions were all wrong. So we ended up throwing them out. Instead, we hired John Jensen who was really wonderful. He's been a storyboard artist for years and years, and has worked with such directors as Cecil B. DeMille, John Ford, Howard Hawks and Alfred Hitchcock - he was just great. John had the kind of technical knowledge that was required, as well as a kind of childlike - and I mean that in a complimentary sense - love of action and adventure. The thing that makes for great movies.\" Jensen and Norton immediately went to work breaking down the script, scene by scene. Story revision was an ongoing process during preproduction - a situation which was to continue well into principal photography in Africa as well -and Jensen's storyboarding skills met with unusual challenges throughout filming. With 'Operation Baby' given the go-ahead, Raponi and his crew began sculpting the clay model from which the rubber dinosaur costume would be created - a reversal of the more customary approach of first hiring the actor who would fit inside and then building the suit around him or her. From this basic form, a lightweight rubber suit was created, complete with a shape-retaining skeleton and points of attachment for the cable-controlled facial, neck and tail mechanisms. Also included were arm extensions which fit into Baby's forelegs, as well as metal supports designed to minimize bending on the part of the operator. Working from the outside in, Raponi began with a mold for the skin. To this he added another one-and-a-half inches for muscle, thus creating a form which would in turn, Baby's parents - scaled down to human size for most of their scenes - were also produced in rubber suit form. / Izzy Raponi examines the fiberglass underside of the partially completed fullsize mother brontosaurus. Countless dinosaur components were fabricated at the Disney Studios and then crated and shipped to the Ivory Coast, where Raponi and his crew - working out of a vacant airplant hangar - made the final assembly. / Michael Reedy and Raponi check out the inner workings of the full-size brontosaurus neck, designed primarily by Ron CINEFEX 22 • 47"],["48","Baby","The brontosaurus family is within the African jungle. In reality, the setting was an intricately detailed quarterscale miniature created on a augmented by outside cable operators - while the tiny in-scale Baby was minimally wire attached to its neck. / Izzy Raponi gives his star a checkup prior to shooting. / A full-size crane-mounted foot and a mechanical tail were employed for an early sequence in which George and Susan Loomis' campsite is inadvertently trampled by first sighted grazing in a primordial clearing deep Disney soundstage by master modelmaker Paul Huston. The adult brontosauri had human performers inside - articulated with a simple a passing brontosaurus. 48 * CINEFEX 22 provide a cast for the inner skeleton. After these basic steps were completed, Raponi could estimate how much room would be left inside for the operator. The mold for Baby's reptilian skin was developed by first encasing the clay sculpture in an Ultracal plaster shell, and then dividing it into smaller sections. The number of sections was determined by the amount of foam latex rubber required, as well as by the actual size of the section that would fit inside the specially designed oven used to cure the rubber. Sectioning was done with a thin metal spacer inserted at designated lines on the sculpture. Plaster was then carefully poured, making certain that no adjoining sections were cast at the same time. The process - which required forty minutes per section to plaster and an additional twenty minutes to dry - resulted in something resembling a giant three-dimensional jigsaw puzzle. Proceeding in this manner, Raponi obtained a full negative mold for the dinosaur's skin. Twenty different sections were made from the Baby sculpture, each numbered and catalogued in sequence -a technique later repeated for the miniature mother and father dinosaurs. Placed inside the negative mold was a three-eighths-inch layer of clay representing the skin, with another inch-and-a-half layer added for musculature. When a final coat of fiber-reinforced Ultracal was applied, the result was a countermold from which the creature's inner skeleton could be fabricated. The skeletal structure was made by coating the countermold with alternating layers of resin polyester and fiberglass. \"The skeleton had to be made of fiberglass,\" said Raponi, \"to make it light enough to be supported by the person inside the suit, and yet strong enough to support the foam muscles and latex skin that would go over it. Metal would have Once the skeleton was constructed, a soft urethane foam supplied Baby with good muscle tone, usually averaging an inch-and-a-half in thickness, but in some spots - depending on the complexity of the movement and the location on the bronto's body - reaching a depth of four inches. The foam was handsculpted over the structure in many areas. Baby's skin was formed by injecting latex foam into the three-eighths-inch gap between the negative mold of the dinosaur and a skin countermold. Each sectionalized mold was cured at 200 degrees for four to six hours in ovens operating at a full twenty-four-hour-a-day capacity in order to meet the scheduled start date. The latex foam used for the dinosaur's skin was a variation of a formula Raponi and Carlo Rambaldi had come across and adapted years earlier while working on Dino De Laurentiis' King Kong. They had spent two months at that time researching a material that approximated flesh and yet satisfied both cosmetic and special effects needs. Developed by veteran Warner Brothers makeup man George Bau, and subsequently manufactured by Charles Schram, the selected material was originally adapted from a Uniroyal formula. Disney makeup supervisor Robert J. Schiffer assisted Raponi in modifying the formula to meet Baby's special needs. For one thing, the skin had to be strong and resilient. The team discovered that by varying the quantities of the ingredients -a latex base, a foaming agent, a curing agent and a gelling solution - the skin's texture and weight could be altered accordingly. Mixing time and room temperature were also contributing factors in creating the latex foam. But making and injecting the substance through the countermold - so as not to mar the skin's exterior surface - and into the space where the clay had been, posed its share of problems. First, the liquid latex was whipped for seventeen minutes to turn it to foam. At that point, Raponi and crew had only thirty seconds at 75-degree room temperature to inject the foam into the mold, making certain meanwhile that it reached all areas within. As the long, hot summer wore on and Burbank temperatures soared to 100 degrees and beyond, this process became even more troublesome. Creating the skin was additionally complicated by the rather hit or miss nature of the curing process. Indeed, there was no real way of knowing whether any given latex section would be suitable until it emerged from the oven. Raponi considered himself fortunate that ninety percent of the castings came out usable. In all, nearly two hundred gallons of liquid latex were used - at a cost of $300 per five-gallon container. Although Baby's latex skin could have been given a base color at the time it was cured, it was decided not to alter the natural navajo white hue. A shooting location had yet to be chosen, and the producers felt that decision might somehow affect the animal's coloring. Also, adding pigment to the rubber might structurally have weakened the skin. The dinosaurs' final skin tones were ultimately determined on location. By early August 1983, the Baby costume was completed to the point where it was possible to cast the 'dino' operators. Auditions were held, and more than 150 candidates turned out for videotape interviews and an opportunity to walk and run around in the Baby test suit. One of the applicants chosen was veteran stuntwoman Paula Crist, who came to the job with an unusual edge, having previously worked as an ape in Planet of the Apes and portrayed a four-armed Ovion in Battlestar Galactica. At five-feet two-inches, Crist was actually taller than the designated height, but managed to land the part by compressing her backbone in order to appear shorter. The other two finalists selected to provide the baby dinosaur's body motion were Terri Girvin - a gymnast, tumbler and former Disneyland costume character - and martial arts expert Richard Aguirre. All three performers had the unexpected common link of either karate or martial arts experience, which endowed them with the mental and physical discipline needed for long stints inside the dark, hot, claustrophobic suits. Their dinosaur training began almost immediately. Special backplates were custom-made for the actors and bolted strategically onto the skeleton of the dinosaur providing stable balance. The devices also allowed a certain amount of freedom, making it possible for the dino people to move their shoulders and lower backs. \"It was important that we got used to being on all fours,\" com-"],["49","Baby","CINEFEX 22 • 49"],["50","Baby","50 - CINEFEX 22"],["51","Baby","mented Crist, \"and that our muscles were well-toned for this particular type of activity. Every maneuver Baby made stressed different muscles in different ways.\" Once a heart and soul had been provided, the final step in bringing Baby to life was mechanizing the suit. Raponi and crew used an extra resin polyester skeleton as a guide to determine the location for the mechanisms, marked the places accordingly and then cut holes. The resin cutouts were used as patterns to measure the exact size for the necessary metal replacement fittings. Raponi credits Leonardo Da Vinci for influencing the simplistic design of his dinosaur mechanisms. \"Da Vinci was a great teacher, and his designs are still applicable today. They were very simple and very practical - and that's what you need in the movie business. Leonardo studied nature and copied it, which is exactly what we did with the dinosaurs for Baby. In real life, movement is produced by muscles which are controlled by the nervous system. Our mechanism is a substitute for muscle, and the cables are substitutes for nerves. The cable-control system is a substitute for the brain. And because the film was shot in remote jungle locations, with extreme temperatures and swampy conditions, we had to rely on practical inventions like cable-controlled pulleys as opposed to high-tech devices. The basic operating mechanism consisted of a pulley, bearings and steel rods. We tried to use brass, aluminum and unoxydizable steel as much as possible because we didn't want anything that could rust. Rust causes traction, making it more likely for the device to break.\" Raponi used motorcycle, bicycle and craft cables at various points, beginning with the smaller cables which were more flexible and better working. After reaching a length of between ten and fifteen feet, a junction box was installed where the smaller cables could be connected to larger ones. For cable control, Raponi's objective was to design a rig that would require the fewest possible number of operators - based on the principle that if one person could do more than one action, his coordination would be better. Normally, four or five cable operators contributed to Baby's performance - two for the neck, and either two or three for the head - depending on what movements were needed. The dinosaur's motivation was decidedly a team effort, with everyone knowing the creature's choreography well ahead of time. This was especially important for the actor inside the suit, as he or she was technically blind. Only a small radio helped to ease breakdowns in communication. In all, Baby boasted fifteen different facial expressions - primarily involving the eyes, eyebrows, nose, lips and mouth. Raponi's crew built a total of seven heads to facilitate these various expressions. Additional mechanisms were set up to provide a chewing motion, as well as making it possible for the tongue to come out and actually lick something. A few sequences, such as those featuring Baby eating, were shot using radio control - a method successfully employed in operating the mouth, eyes and eyelids. Even with the radio-controlled model, however, it was necessary for the major neck movements The rubber suits were split down the middle on the underside, allowing access for the operator whose hands and feet fit into the dinosaur's legs and whose head was positioned at the base of the neck. Paula Crist - one of three people engaged to wear the Baby costumes - crouches in position as dinosaur wranglers prepare to place the suit over her. / A battery of cable-operators provided Baby with tail, head and facial articulations. / Susan Loomis (Sean Young) makes an offering of friendship and food. / Izzy Raponi with two of his Baby suits. / Raponi makes a last-minute touchup as Baby is prepared for her underwater scenes, filmed in a tank on the Disney lot during postproduction. CINEFEX 22 • 51"],["52","Baby","Bill Norton blocks out the action for a scene in which Baby and her mother share a repast gathered for them by Crist - the designated suit physically taxing, in-suit time was generally limited to an hour or less between operated head and neck were used to represent the mother brontosaurus. The scene, shot on stage at Disney, was photographed against a blue George and Susan. Paula operator for the scene - waits in the background. Since performing in the cumbersome outfits was rest breaks. / A cranemounted, hydraulicallyscreen for later optical compositing with jungle footage filmed in Africa. 52 • CINEFEX 22 to be hand-controlled since no servomotor was strong enough to overcome its weight. One avenue for the expression of Baby's personality and character could not be explored until postproduction, however - the development of a suitable dinosaur voice. Assigned to the task were sound effects technicians Mark Mangini and George Budd of Thundertracks, Ltd. \"In the script,\" said Budd, \"every time the dinosaur opened its mouth, it said 'Haroo,' no matter what emotion it was supposed to express - mourning its dead father or chasing the bad guy.\" It was Thundertracks' job to translate the written 'Haroos' into real sounds. \"Our approach,\" said Mangini, \"was not to imagine what a dinosaur should sound like. We tried instead to imagine what would be the most dramatic. We began by listening to lots and lots of different animals and assembling sounds that seemed like they might come from a dinosaur.\" Mangini and Budd eventually combined more than two dozen different animal sounds to create Baby's voice. \"One of the challenges of the film,\" explained Budd, \"was that you had this creature that was on screen as much as any of the principals, and who had to have a character. So she had to have an emotional range. We were looking for expressive sounds, sounds that conveyed some sort of emotion.\" Among the sounds finally selected were those of a whale, a raccoon, a turkey and even an asthmatic cat. Generally, the various noises were not mixed simultaneously - except in two sequences - but were instead cut together in quick succession. The adult dinosaurs were given different, but similar, voices. \"We thought it would be nice if there was some form of correspondence between the parents and Baby,\" commented Budd, \"so that you would believe they belonged to the same family.\" By late August, Jonathan Taplin and Bill Norton had returned from a location scouting trip with the triumphant discovery of the perfect setting. After visiting several countries including Kenya, Gabon and Cameroon - where Baby cinematographer John Alcott had recently shot Greystoke - veteran Disney producer and cameraman Jack Couffer suggested they check out the Ivory Coast. The politically stable West African nation offered a reasonably good network of paved roads, wide rivers - which were called for in the script - and an abundance of rain forest jungle. Both producer and director felt there was much to be gained by filming in authentic African locations - especially in the area of assisting audiences in their suspension of disbelief - and the Ivory Coast seemed ideally lush and exotic. \"We could never have shot Baby on the backlot,\" commented Taplin. \"My problem with King Kong is that it just doesn't look like Africa. Even the extras don't look African. Africans have a very distinct look to them. You can't go to Watts and recruit people and pretend they're Africans. It just doesn't work.\" Once a location had been approved, production efforts increased in momentum, and a principal photography start date was set for December 5, 1983 - with the dinosaurs scheduled to be crated and shipped by an alarmingly precipitous October 7. Since Norton had only decided on the final size of Baby in June, and the 'dino people' hadn't been selected until August, the effects crew had a scant month-and-a-half to completely redesign and fabricate the film's main attraction. To expedite the process, Baby's dinosaur construction team worked in assembly line fashion to manufacture the requisite seven Baby suits. \"I had five people working with me,\" Raponi recalled. \"One supervised the rubber room, one was in charge of making skeletons and muscles, one person made the mechanical head, one did the neck and another worked on the tail.\" But in spite of the well-organized efforts, the physical distance between the various shops caused a frustrating loss of precious time. \"I had asked to have one unit shop, or at least a stage where we could all work together. That way, the job would have been much easier to control and assembly would have been simplified. Instead, we had to walk between five different shops no less than seven or ten times a day. It created a great deal of mental stress and made the whole project more difficult. Also, we had to transport all the molds a fair distance to the ovens - so we devised a dolly with a cushion on it to prevent the rubber from collapsing in transit.\" Even after the assorted components were finished - including the head, skeleton, muscles and skin - Raponi and crew had yet to face the formidable task of assembling the creature. Beginning with Baby's musculature, three or four different hand-carved layers of the urethane foam were attached to the fiberglass skeleton at strategic points where movement was minimal. Each layer was separated by silk to ensure a more fluid motion than could be achieved by a single block of the material - which, in turn, maximized elasticity for extension and contraction purposes. The foam muscles were then covered by sections of outer skin glued together to close and conceal seams. The animal's actual visible layer of skin was a pigmented rubber cement base added while on location in Africa. The design of Baby's eyes was the very last item on the bronto-building agenda - and as little as one month before the scheduled departure date, there was still disagreement among the producers, writers, art directors, special effects staff and director as to just what look, shape and color they should have. A major concern was to avoid any similarity to the well-known round eyes of E.T. - even though most reptilian eyes are indeed round. In the end, a narrow almond-shaped feline orb - featuring a vertical pupil and green iris, with small amounts of yellow, green and blue on the sides, surrounded by lots of white - was decided upon by committee. \"This was a major problem,\" said Raponi. \"The life of any animal is in its eyes; and since the movement of cat eyes is very limited, that particular shape doesn't allow for subtle expressions. Because the shape is pointy, the eye cannot go up or down - only sideways. This shape also dictated the mechanism required to control it. Most large animals, like cows and elephants, have round pupils; and because of the shape of their"],["53","Baby","faces, it's necessary for them to turn their entire head to see left or right. You can see the life in their eyes by the little bit of white that surrounds the iris. With Baby, there was too much white; and we had to exaggerate movements in order to suggest that it was seeing anything at all. This extraneous action made it look ridiculous. I would have chosen a round pupil for the dinosaur.\" John Scheele concurred. \"The eye is the window to the soul. It can grab you, suck you in and captivate you. Baby's eyes were a disaster. You weren't drawn in by their appeal; and because of the artificial color, there was a problem with credibility. A more intricate eye was called for - perhaps something with a dilating Raponi had wanted to use an optical specialist outside the studio to lend a professional touch in fabricating Baby's eyes; but because of the time constraints, the job had to be done in-house. And although the Disney paint department was somewhat more experienced in painting walls than producing the fine detail work necessary for lifelike eyeballs, there were no other options available. Raponi put the crew to work by first having the eyes painted on a piece of paper, and then photographed to smooth out the color and give the rendering an overall finer grain. Lastly, the finished product was placed inside transparent plastic spheres filled with transparent resin to create the suggestion of a lens. While much of the preproduction attention had been focused on the most obvious and basic element of Baby - the construction of its dinosaur stars - progress was being made in other areas as well. Considerations for the visual aspects of the film had been weighed and discussed; and on the optical effects front, the fundamentals of photography were pondered at length. For Academy Award-winning cinematographer John Alcott, the choice was simple. Fresh from shooting the visually elegant Greystoke, the director of photography was anxious to implement his pioneered system, Supertechniscope - a widescreen, but nonanamorphic process compatible with Kodak's high-speed 5294 film as well as a wide range of Arriflex high-speed lenses. This process - which uses only about two-and-a-half perfs of the film's four-perf height - had proven a real advantage while filming in the low light levels of the African jungle, but on Baby was to create serious problems with the compositing of optical Special effects cinematographer Peter Anderson, who was responsible for bluescreen and optical work on the film, expressed early concerns with the Supertechniscope process, suggesting the picture be shot instead in a flat 1.85 aspect ratio with optical elements being photographed on full aperture 4-perf and reduced to conform during compositing. At one point, Anderson and assistant director Steve McEveety had even looked into the possibility of using Digital Productions' advanced computer equipment for shooting and processing the bluescreen shots. Tests were made implementing magenta and blue screens for mattes, but it was eventually discovered that the conventional and much-used blue was still the best choice. CINEFEX 22 • 53"],["54","Baby","54 • CINEFEX 22"],["55","Baby","In the end, Alcott's Supertechniscope system was given the go-ahead - with the subsidiary employment of VistaVision for the effects elements, a decision which offered at least some compromise to the optical production dilemma. Production staffers immediately went to work lining up the only two high-speed VistaVision cameras available - capable of filming up to 72 frames per second -and prepping them for location work. A third, Disney-owned 30-frame-per-second VistaVision camera was also readied. Although the variable highspeed capacity of the venerable equipment was required in order to give Baby's miniatures the illusion of weight and size, some modification was needed. The out-of-date 1953/1954 rigs arrived on the lot sporting poorly mismatched sets of mounts and lenses which were inferior to contemporary optics, and had to be converted to accept more appropriate Nikon issue - thus eliminating any ensuing resolution problems. Anderson and Phil Meador, head of Disney's special photographic effects department, were also concerned about using the Kodak 5294 film for opticals. Much testing was done, and although it was found that this stock was excellent for low light level filming, it was not at all appropriate for bluescreen. The high-speed film simply did not lend itself to making quality separations, and so the slower 5247 stock was consequently selected for all bluescreen foreground plate photography. While preproduction wrinkles were frantically being ironed out, preparations were underway to relocate the Baby entourage in the African rain forest - a major production in itself, and one marked by a few real-life script changes. One noteworthy alteration was the last-minute announcement by Tom Wilhite that he was leaving the Disney organization to enter independent production. Richard Berger replaced him as Baby's executive producer just as the final provisions for location shooting were being made. Another example of the best laid plans of mice, men and dinosaurs going awry was the surprise cancellation of Peter Anderson's location participation. The effects specialist had planned to join the crew several weeks into principal photography with the intention of shooting the essential optical scenes. But less than forty hours before Anderson and his assistant, Richard Mosier, were to depart for location, plans were changed. The opticals would now be photographed back at the studio, but the miniatures, plates and composite planning would still be done in Africa. With no optical specialist on site, John Jensen and VistaVision technician John Godden were tasked with filling in as best they could. By mid-November, Izzy Raponi and his five-person team had arrived in Abidjan, the Ivory Coast's capital city. A 'dino base' was readied for them in a hangar at the local airport, and the crew began setting up the shop where much of the film's dinosaur construction would be completed. It was soon apparent, however, that the efforts of the American craftsmen were simply not enough to accomplish the herculean tasks that lay ahead, and it became necessary for Raponi to seek assistance from the neighborhood. Ten local Ivorians were brought in to Father dinosaur peers tentatively into the primordial mists. The Dinosaur Lake set - actually a convincing Paul Huston miniature - was dressed with living plants by greensman Phil Hoescht. / While technicians prepare the patriarch for an ensuing shot, a pipe support is braced at the base of the creature's neck - thus offering a moment's respite for the aching back and neck muscles of the 'dino person' within. / Studio painter Shelly Phillips compares a reference photo of African banyan trees - taken on location by Huston - to the quarter-scale recreations constructed on stage. A small cardboard cutout man positioned in the background aided the miniatures crew in determining a proper sense of proportion. / While an operator provided momentum from inside the animal, externally-controlled cables articulated most of the neck and head movement. Baby's enraged father charging through the jungle was filmed at highspeed to enhance the illusion of weight and mass. CINEFEX 22 • 55"],["56","Baby","As the crane-operated father begins his rampage, Kiviat's simpering assistant, Nigel Jenkins (Julian Fellowes), and his troop of African mercenaries flee in terror. / A mud-spattered Baby leans rapturously against her surrogate mother. With the skilled assistance of four or five cable operators, the dinosaur hatchling was capable of fifteen different facial expressions. / Baby comes face to face with a family of elephants and cautiously sizes up the similarities. The scene of the temporarily-orphaned infant seeking refuge among these large mammals was shot on the Disney backlot after the main unit had returned from Africa. Ultimately, however, it was cut from the film. 56 • CINEFEX 22 help - and as it turned out, also provided a certain amount of hindrance as well. The fact that most of the natives spoke only French, causing numerous communication difficulties, was just the beginning of the problems. More troubling was the local custom of napping on the job - a situation not uncommon in hot tropical climates where the diet is generally meager. Raponi tackled the challenge head-on by setting up a kitchen at the dino base and cooking nutritious pasta dishes for his staff. The level of productivity increased dramatically from that point on and siestas became a thing of the past. Raponi's ingenious solution spawned yet another unforeseen complication, however. The number of mouths to feed grew mysteriously larger each day as the Ivorian workers spread the word to their friends and relatives. Chaos and confusion reigned almost from the beginning of the African tour of duty - and continued throughout. One of the more baffling sources of consternation was the continually changing script, which was altered almost daily as director Norton, storyboard artist Jensen, script polisher David Freeman and actors William Katt and Sean Young met to discuss the Some of the decisions made at these conferences had rather far-reaching effects, particularly for Raponi and his long-suffering crew. One of the more dramatic changes included the alteration of the sixty-foot mother dinosaur's position from the designed and partially executed standing version to one required to float down the river while prone. And, as the script had originally called for the matriarch to be transported via helicopter, the addition of a now-requisite raft to the brontosaurus crew's job list created extra burdens. An unforeseen complication arose when the time came to weld the metal raft. Accomplishing the task at the already-established dino base would have created a major safety hazard due to the close proximity of the flammable rubber cement-based dye being used to color the dinosaurs. It therefore became necessary to establish a second shop. Unfortunately, that facility turned out to be twenty miles away on the other side of town - a situation which added the new and exciting realm of poor or nonexistent communication between the dino bases. The crew had already been beleaguered with seemingly endless setbacks in obtaining the proper brontosaurus pigment. The formula Raponi had tested with success in Southern California proved disastrous in the sweltering humidity and 100degree temperatures of the Ivory Coast. The latex foam skin of the great beasts absorbed the salt and water from the air and created a bubbling chemical reaction when the colored cement was applied. Raponi solved this dilemma by purchasing a locally available brand of glue which was especially designed for humid climates. True to course, the glue - ironically imported from Italy - would not mix with the American pigments. Local dyes were the next item on Raponi's shopping list: and once they were obtained, the problem was at last resolved. Africa's Ivory Coast offered an extraordinary scenic beauty found few other places on earth. But the lush primordial setting came hand-in-hand with the myriad of corresponding hardships intrinsic to any primitive location - and Baby suffered accordingly. One of the facts of life discovered during the African sojourn was the inevitability of daily power blackouts. Because the Ivory Coast - and much of Africa - was in the midst of an ongoing drought, and since the bulk of its electrical energy was derived from hydroelectric generators, power cuts were predictably unpredictable and frequent. It was not uncommon, in fact, to be without electricity for up to twelve hours a day. While this had little effect on the actual filming - where portable power generators were employed as standard practice - it did, however, have a decidedly adverse impact on operations at the dino bases. In order to meet the rigid shooting deadlines, Raponi and crew often found themselves applying a final coat of paint to Baby's skin by flashlight, sometimes as late as two o'clock in the morning. Matching colors under these circumstances was difficult at best. Obtaining supplies and replacement parts in the Ivory Coast presented additional problems. It had taken weeks for the 85 crates of camera equipment and 28 crates of dinosaur parts to filter through massive bureaucratic red tape upon arrival in Africa. Once there, even such standard items as screws or nails could prove elusive. Although the effects team had done their best to anticipate all possible needs beforehand, inevitable shortages occurred. One crisis that evolved from inadequate stock happened quite by accident - the full-size mother dinosaur was inadvertently painted with a pigment-and-water solution instead of the necessary rubber cement. In the process, large quantities of the precious pigment were wasted; and in the end, the color simply brushed off. Raponi had to scramble to find more of the local pigment needed to rectify the situation. The long working days and the difficult conditions took their toll on the crew. A week-and-a-half after his African arrival, Raponi himself contracted a mild case of what was believed to be malaria - despite his taking the prescribed anti-malaria pills - and spent several unpleasant days out of commission. Other members of the crew were dropping like flies. A couple of days in bed with an intravenous diet was the usual remedy for an ailment, and the local doctor assigned to the film had his hands full. While the dinosaur construction crew raced against the clock to finish the creatures for filming, Crist, Girvin and Aguirre began the rigorous training necessary to bring Baby to life under the difficult African conditions. \"I was somewhat prepared for the intense heat,\" said Crist, \"because I had experience as a dancer in the Caribbean and had also worked in suits before. When I first arrived in the Ivory Coast, I started practicing every day by walking with the dinosaur suit's front leg extensions. I got into my black hooded leotard and put a heavy sweat suit on top of that in order to simulate conditions inside the suit. This helped my body to adjust aerobically to the heat and also to build an endurance so I wouldn't sweat so much. I did this at the"],["57","Baby","CINEFEX 22 • 57"],["58","Baby","58 • CINEFEX 22"],["59","Baby","hotel and also on some of our jungle locations, just to get the feel of it. African children would gather around and watch me like I was crazy as I scrambled around on all fours.\" The black hooded outfit the dino people wore was a custommade, one-piece unitard. The half-cotton/half-nylon blend was carefully designed to absorb perspiration while at the same time offering maximum stretch. Since the Baby suits had little or no ventilation - a built-in air conditioning system had been tested back in the States, but proved unworkable - this costume served to create a natural means of cooling the performers. To enhance the heat reduction process, the dinosaur operators were drenched with room temperature water before entering the suit. The moist leotard next to the body reduced evaporation by fifty percent; and with a limited loss of body fluids, it was easier to keep cool. Additionally, an extra dose of oxygen was available through an opening in the bottom of the foam latex suit. It was not uncommon for the temperature inside the costume to reach an unbearable 140 degrees while on location. And although the average length of time spent within was between thirty and forty-five minutes, the performers occasionally had to endure the suit's oppression for as long as an hour. Paula Crist learned to deal with the cruelly hot confines by making resourceful use of mental imagery. \"Your mind controls everything. So I pictured myself in a cool wide-open space with lots of beautiful flowers and a constant breeze. Even so, no matter how prepared one is for being in a claustrophobic suit like Baby, it's different when you're actually in there. It's dark, the air is thin and dank and the suit itself is touching and squeezing you all over. I still got claustrophobic at times, but I just had to fight it off.\" Raponi completed the final preparations for the seven incarnations of Baby - from the lightweight running-only model to the burdensome mechanical rendition - as well as completing the two full-size parent bodies, which were sixteen feet tall and twenty-eight feet long, excluding the neck and tail. Work on the miniature adult brontos was also underway. But the dinosaur detail was only one area of concern. In the last frantic days before filming started, there was great activity in all camps. Jensen and Norton filled numerous storyboards with ambitious action sequences and planned optical shots involving the miniature mother and father. Meanwhile, production designer Ray Storey searched for last-minute locations and supervised the construction of sets and site preparation. One of the most important sites employed during the filming was 'Dinosaur Lake' - used for the key scene in which the good guys do battle with the bad guys for possession of the amazing animals. The primeval swampy area was discovered during an aerial survey of the jungle region near Abidjan, and an eightmile road with a bridge was eventually built in order to accommodate the army of vehicles needed by the film company. Initially, however, because the lake did not appear on any existing maps, Storey and his scouting team first had to pinpoint the location and then work their way in by land. After stopping at the village nearest the lake - actually named Lake Ingrakon - and palavering with the chief, it was learned that this particular body of water was considered sacred. A bottle of rum was passed around in the spirit of friendship, with a generous swig poured into the lake for its resident deities. After numerous meetings, negotiating sessions and substantially more rum, the chief and village witch doctor agreed to 'desacrilize' the lake for a period of one year in order to guarantee the safety of the film crew. A shopping list of the sacrifices needed to appease the gods included goats, chickens, rice and a size thirteen pair of white shoes. Disney gladly paid the price of these items and asked no questions. Elsewhere, in the former capital city of Grand Bassam, some thirty miles from Abidjan, an entire village was being constructed in a jungle clearing. More than fifty mud huts sprang up almost overnight to dress a set that was to become known as 'Destruction Village' - for it was here that the climactic scenes of the rampaging mother dinosaur were to take place. Most of the authentic-looking structures were made of breakaway materials, and curious natives from neighboring villages watched as the odd new housing development took form. Dodging deadly green mambas became a matter of course for the English construction workers as the snakes sought refuge from the intense African sun by hiding in the cool shade under the huts. Another location selected for Baby was the thick jungle in Parc National du Banco, a national park near Abidjan. Because of its lush foliage and natural camouflage, this spot was not only ideally suited to filming, but was also a notorious hideout for local bandits. Fortunately, the production suffered no added grief because of this; and whether or not any famous African desperadoes actually observed the goings-on was never known. In addition to principal photography, an ambitious scheme was devised to shoot all of the miniature and bluescreen work on location. Although such work is traditionally rendered under more regulated conditions during postproduction, it was thought that by photographing the same unobstructed skylines and brush used in the live-action sequences, the overall look of the movie would be enhanced. Another considered advantage was that the quality of light throughout the film would have been the same, thus eliminating any problems in matching the color and softness. To assist with the miniature effects, ILM miniatures expert Paul Huston was approached just as he was *a tempted Doom. finishing up work on Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom. Baby seemed just the change of pace he needed, and Huston joined the crew in Africa two short weeks later. \"When I first got there, I visited all the sets with construction manager Len Furey and saw what had been done so far. This gave me an idea of how things looked so I could reproduce them in miniature. I also spent some time with John Jensen getting his basic ideas about the kind of action the story called for. John revealed things to me a little bit at a time, based on the storyboarding that had been done in Africa. It was quite a bit different from what they had left the States with.\" It was during this time that Huston first learned about the Captured by the ruthless Kiviat, Baby's mother is drugged and bound for transport to civilization. Izzy Raponi carefully applies a cosmetic touch-up to the dormant dinosaur. The delicate foam latex skin - especially susceptible to deterioration in the hot, humid African climate - required constant maintenance. Plastic coverings protected sensitive surfaces when not in use. / Though designed and built to be used in a standing position, the full-size brontosaurus form had to be modified and reconfigured in Africa to accommodate a script change which called for it to be transported via raft rather than helicopter. / The 'victorious' captors en route to Destruction Village with mother in tow. CINEFEX 22 • 59"],["60","Baby","Baby's foster parents offer momentary comfort before resuming their flight from Kiviat's swiftly approaching George make a furtive and futile dockside attempt to release the noble beast from its bonds. / Baby cast, crew and creatures on location at Dinosaur Lake. The primeval marshy area was located a secluded eight miles away occasionally-impassable specifically by the film crew for conveyance to the site. helicopter. / Susan and from Abidjan, and was accessible only by a frequently-muddy and road and bridge - built 60 4 CINEFEX 22 plans for a miniature 'Destruction Village' and the extent of the additional jungle miniatures that would be needed. Jensen also revealed to him the attendant practical effects which called for the dinosaurs to crash through the jungle, breaking down trees and sets, and for the stricken father dinosaur to fall into the water and then come up again. \"I thought they were kidding about building miniatures in the middle of the jungle,\" said Huston. \"I had never really heard of shooting miniatures on location. This kind of thing is usually done in the studio under controlled conditions. Dinosaur Lake was hard enough to get to, let alone bringing in all the material necessary to build sets. It took over an hour just to drive there - and you were lucky if you made it through the last twenty minutes, it was so muddy and treacherous.\" It didn't take long for Huston to convince producer Taplin and company that building the miniatures on actual locations could prove to be a real nightmare. The various rustic settings were difficult enough for full-size filming, and the precise and exacting photography required for the small models would be excruciating. Based on this evaluation, the decision was made to produce all miniature effects and bluescreen work back home in Los Angeles where conditions were more predictable. Before returning, however, Huston spent considerable time collecting necessary reference data. To measure the trees and brush he would later be recreating in the studio, Huston was aided by an improvised Third World measuring device - a fifteen-foot bamboo pole marked off with white tape at one-foot increments. With his African interpreter standing next to various trees and foliage, the miniature consultant took dozens of photographs which would later provide him with a sense of scale and help to determine the height and width of his miniature reproduction. These unique trees - the beautiful gnarly banyans with their complex roots visible above ground - proved challenging to build, even in relatively large one-fourth scale. Baby actually began filming on December 5, 1983. The main attraction, however, did not make her debut until eight days later, affording Raponi a little extra time to deal with the idiosyncracies of African life. When Baby's start date finally arrived, and cast and crew were assembled in a remote jungle setting promptly for their early morning call, the production faced but one problem - Baby was nowhere to be found. While the frantic Raponi envisioned his mechanical offspring being kidnapped and spirited away to neighboring Ghana, a search ensued for the absent artifact. Four long hours later, the African driver responsible for chauffeuring the dinosaur star sheepishly arrived on the scene. He had gotten lost somewhere en route and decided to take a nap. Later that same auspicious day, Baby was ready for action - sort of. As the cameras began grinding, Baby not only took her first step into movie history, but into a giant tree as well. The human-operated, mechanically controlled dinosaur suit that had performed so well during numerous tests on the level workshop floors proved unsteady at best on the rough African turf. The crew attempted to battle the wobbles by flattening and preparing a path beforehand, but this proved generally impractical given the pressures of limited time. \"You couldn't really prep the ground in the jungle,\" explained Paula Crist. \"It had deep roots, grass, leaves and mud. The only way to deal with it was to practice walking the ground on the extensions, and to use our feet like shock absorbers. We had to learn to rock without losing our balance.\" Aside from the difficult balancing act, another problem that surfaced immediately was the inadequacy of the communications device linking the effects operators to the person inside the dinosaur suit. Once the costumed performer began to perspire, water got in the microphone/receiver, causing static. The dense jungle surroundings also contributed to the interference. The only feasible solution was to rely more on rehearsals than on the radio device. The dino people practiced by walking through a scene - sometimes with their eyes shut - to get a sense of the action and the layout of the set. Radio communication was still used sparingly, with Ron Tantin guiding maneuvers by giving such vocal commands as turn left, skitter back, crab right' and so on. Communications was only one aspect of Baby's many operational quandaries. Coordinating the external controls with the internal movements provided an altogether different set of problems. While the performer within the latex suit generated the major physical action, all neck, head and facial controls were manipulated externally by Raponi and crew. The dino person could only anticipate the external motivation, a situation complicated by the irregularly staffed effects team. And because of the hectic shooting schedule, Raponi and his stalwart band were not permitted the luxury of experimentation or muchneeded rehearsal time. \"Another big problem,\" said Raponi, \"was that the person controlling the cable mechanism had to feel the part and be an actor or it would seem terrible. It is not the cable, but the animator who controls it that makes the creature live. His hand and his brain give the emotion and timing of the movement. In some scenes, the eyes were moving too fast while in others they were not moving enough. On top of that, my crew was split apart after we had spent more than two months preparing.\" Raponi's specialized crew of creature constructors and operators was frequently back at the dino base doing emergency repairs, or - even more aggravatingly - called away from cable-operating duties to provide smoke effects or explosions in other areas. Native laborers filled in at the control panels, contributing to Baby's somewhat uneven The Baby-costumed actors were able to contribute a certain amount of additional assistance to the cable operators, and suffered their share of discomfort as a result. \"Our heads were located at the base of Baby's neck,\" explained Crist. \"And since I was taller, I had an advantage because my head went a little further into the neck which made lifting it easier. I could also help turn the head. The cables could only pull so much and we"],["61","Baby","CINFFEY 22 • 61"],["62","Baby",""],["63","Baby","had to add the extra lift to the neck or head ourselves.\" The weight of the dinosaur's head was magnified several times at the base of the neck, however, and caused constant backaches for the dino people trying to support it. They succeeded in taking some of the strain off by slightly arching or rocking themselves back as a counterbalance - thereby creating sore muscles in the leg and buttock region. And due to the tremendous weight of the costume, the performers' hands - which of necessity leaned on Baby's front leg extensions - were also plagued by discomfort. \"Even though the extensions were padded and molded to our own grips, our hands would get tired. Sometimes it felt as if someone were putting a knife through my palm.\" Despite all the creature discomforts, being a dino person in the exotic African rain forest was not without its gratification. And troopers that they were, the three Baby performers couldn't wait for their turn in the suit. Each developed his or her own specialty; and whether it was expressed by stunts, running or just plain walking, Baby's buddies offered unique contributions rendered under the most difficult of circumstances. In spite of the heroic efforts of Baby's building and operating crews, the brontosaurus was replete with shortcomings. The resulting delays and setbacks served to exasperate Bill Norton, and the director was forced to improvise when actions fell short of expectations - a situation arising all too frequently. \"It wasn't at all like directing an actor. With actors you can have them go in, hit their marks, say their lines and walk out - and you've got it. Here there were a lot of variables. The cables would break; the dinosaur would get a scratch. It wouldn't look right, or wouldn't get the right expression. The fully cabled machanical Baby had a walking range of about six feet - in a straight line. If it had to make any movements, if it had to go left or right, it could maybe go two steps forward, two steps to the left, and that was about it. Closeups were much easier to shoot because there wasn't a lot of movement involved. Normally you could get them in one or two takes. But for a shot where Baby would have to walk three feet up and move to the left and swing her head around, that would require up to fifteen or twenty takes. I usually had this grandiose idea of what Baby's action would be - like getting up and walking through the door. But once we started to shoot the scene, we'd realize that we could only get certain movements in such and such a way. The odd thing is that it really comes off well in the film. It's amazing. During the editing process, when you aren't dealing with the out-takes, you begin to think of it as more of a character or a living creature. \"One of the challenges that I faced as a director was finding ways to make certain scenes work. We had to switch scenes around because we were limited by what the dinosaur could or couldn't do. A scene where the dinosaur falls in a ditch and they have to pull it up could be very difficult to shoot depending on how it was staged. There were things I wanted to do that I couldn't because of the limitations imposed by the fact that Baby was a mechanical creature. For instance, two scenes come to mind. One is a scene where George and Susan have to lasso Baby, more or less, and she drags them around the campsite. I realized that there was no way we could get that kind of action out of the dinosaur. So I came up with a scene where George and Susan play the mother dinosaur's voice on a tape recorder and Baby is attracted to it. They have the recorder on a string, and they pull the string into the tent. Baby follows it, then George goes in and tries to attach a transmitter onto her. Baby goes berserk and takes off running. There's a big chase, she runs into a stream, George falls over, and there's a lot of splashing and action. That was much easier to film because we were able to get two guys running - holding onto each other the way they do in burlesque theatre when two guys play a horse - but with the tent covering them, it gave the illusion of Baby. It was something we could shoot, it was easy to do and it ended up being very \"Another example was the scene when George and Susan first meet Baby. They get her away from her parents, and she comes swimming up in a pond. George then dives under. Suddenly they see each other, and scare each other. It's a very amusing scene. Originally, it was written to be played on land with lots of action. But it was just too difficult to get that kind of movement out of the dinosaur, so we had to make adjustments.\" \"Politics and movies are the art of compromise,\" Jonathan Taplin added. \"You push and push for perfection, but you also have to realize that you have a schedule. You have a certain amount of time to make a movie and a budget to make it on. There are simply some things you can do and some things you can't - and you have to learn to accept that. Having a guy like Norton was wonderful, because he's a writer, too. As a result, he was always able to adapt the script to our ability to do things. He was great at that.\" Switching much of the action to the water was a simple solution to Baby's unsteady performance on land. The water was more forgiving and disguised some of the awkwardness that occurred on the rough and uneven ground. To devise the necessary plot alterations, Norton and Jensen worked long hours storyboarding water sequences - much to the chagrin of Katt and Young who were now consigned to the dirty, parasitic and snake-infested rivers of the Ivory Coast for much of their African stay. And of course, the new storyline created additional problems for the already beleaguered effects crew. Raponi's original plans had provided for only a limited number of water scenes, with those few taking place at the end of the filming schedule. The increased need for an aquatic animal wreaked unforeseen havoc on Baby's foam latex skin and the pigmented rubber cement outer coating. Raponi was constantly mending, patching and painting the fragile dinosaur surface -and suffered the additional complications of rusting cables and mechanisms as well. And despite the intentional simplicity of Raponi's designs, the necessary repairs were frustratingly time-consuming. Quite frequently, the only hours available to mend and service the dinosaurs were in the middle of the night: and the crew worked virtually seven days a week - including Christmas and New A crane-mounted camera looks down on the mother brontosaurus as she is finally released from her bonds. The tractor-mounted mechanical neck, with an accompanying partial back section, could be used from certain select angles to suggest the beast in its full form. A subsequent shot of the dinosaur as she struggles free and begins her wild rampage through the village involved optically combining the miniature mother and dock with liveaction background material shot on location. CINEFEX 22 • 63"],["64","Baby","climactic Destruction Village sequence, footage of Baby's rampaging mother was shot costumed 'dino person' on through the streets of the miniature village. The Paul Huston set - replicated with assistance from Disney prop shop manager Bob Spetter - was a detailed replica of the full-size village constructed Jamie Anderson - skillfully maneuvered by grips - refers to a video monitor in order upside-down VistaVision rig mounted beneath him. As dinosaurs, the sequence was second to create the illusion of scale and weight. / The unsuccessfully tries to get brontosaurus hatchling was affixed to a rocking platform and photographed against a advancing mother - were then composited with actor Patrick McGoohan inside the As an element for the against a blue screen for later compositing. / Assistant director Steve McEveety urges the on location in Africa. / Recumbent cameraman to operate the modified with most of the scenes featuring the miniature shot at sixty frames per truck in which Kiviat away with the priceless blue screen. / Miniature background plates of the enraged - and rapidly truck cab. 64 • CINEFEX 22 For the performers inside the dinosaur suit, the water scenes made an already taxing situation even more of a challenge. \"Walking in water was extremely difficult,\" recalled Crist. \"Besides the suction factor, each of the feet would fill up and weigh about fifty pounds. With the weight of the costume itself, that added up to about 250 pounds. The water would come up to our elbows sometimes, and the mud and silt would collect in the bottom of the feet. And then the director would say, 'Go faster!' On top of all that, the soggy costume smelled just awful.\" Crew members stood by with blow dryers to ready the wet suits for future scenes. For the land sequences, it was decided that Baby needed an application of mud to take the shine off her latex skin. \"For a while,\" said Norton, \"we just threw mud on her in every scene. Then we discovered that the mud ended up photographing white and looked bad. Unfortunately, we didn't actually discover this for a couple of weeks because the film was being sent off to London for processing and the dailies took that long to reach us. We had to reshoot some of the footage. The rest we covered up with a continuity scene where Baby jumps in a mudpile.\" By late December, VistaVision technician John Godden arrived on location to assist in photographing the background plates essential to postproduction opticals. But because of the massive confusion and the absence of an optical supervisor, Godden spent five weeks on location and the vintage high-speed cameras - so carefully prepared by Peter Anderson - were never used. Instead the slower, 30-frame-per-second production VistaVision camera was put into service towards the end of the African stay. First unit photography was completed by February 1984, and a grateful crew bid adieu to the Ivory Coast and its assortment of snakes, scorpions and other insects, exotic illnesses and weather problems. Second unit director and cinematographer Egil Woxholt - a veteran of several James Bond films and the underwater photographer on Das Boot - remained behind to shoot additional scenes with Baby, as well as plates for compositing. Paul Huston had left for home several weeks earlier to begin preparing the miniature sets back at the Disney Studios. In Los Angeles, plans were underway to photograph the miniatures outdoors in order to simulate the African lighting ambience. However, no suitable unobstructed views resembling the Ivorian landscape could be found, so Huston had to resort to building his miniature sets on soundstages. The first order of business was to construct a convincing replica of the large primeval Dinosaur Lake - a set giving Huston quite a bit of leeway since most of the action in Africa was shot from the beach looking out to the lake, while the majority of the miniature set action featured the beach and shore which were never really seen before. The decision to build the reproductions in one-quarter scale provided additional artistic freedom for the miniature consultant. \"At ILM, we usually work in a scale smaller than one-eighth. In fact, one-eighth was the largest I had ever"],["65","Baby","CINEFEX 22 • 65"],["66","Baby","previously worked in. The one-quarter scale was naturalistic and really great to work with. For one thing, it meant we could use live plants. We began by creating a fictitious-shaped lake and beach which resembled the original in Africa. Matching the trees on the beach was really tough; but once again, we could take certain liberties. We found some gnarly tree branches with a similar look and the right scale and, by piecing them together, assembled the full one-quarter scale trees- complete with their weird roots and trunks. Our greensman, Phil Hoescht, did a terrific job matching the indigenous African growth in The next construct to rise from the soundstage floor was a likeness of Destruction Village. Disney's prop shop manager, Bob Spetter, lent enormous assistance by recreating the rustic hamlet originally constructed in the Ivorian jungle. \"The miniature set for Destruction Village,\" continued Huston, \"involved closely reproducing an entire street with nearly twenty buildings. The breakaway huts were made of foaming plaster - or Tyrocell - with mud-covered reed backing. We had to experiment with the wetness of the mud in order to get the correct amount of cracking we needed. The non-breakaway structures were made of plywood, with bundles of reed tied together with wire strands and then stapled on.\" The Destruction Village sequence actually provided one of the dinosaur's best optical moments - thanks to a special camera rig devised by Peter Anderson and John Godden to effectively film the difficult scene. \"We did something that no one had ever done before,\" said Anderson. \"We took the highspeed VistaVision camera and turned it upside down to film this scene. The VistaVision camera with its steerable gearhead - or friction/fluid head - is about nineteen inches from the bottom, where it bolts on, up to the lens. This was impractical for shooting the village where, in scale, you had to be six inches above the ground - simulating Sean Young's point of view from the back of a motorcycle. A trench wouldn't have worked, so we turned the camera upside down. By inverting the camera, we had to deal only with the space above the lens, which was two-and-a-half to three inches. With opticals, it didn't matter that we were shooting upside down since it could be corrected during the compositing. In the thirty-year history of the camera, no one had any remembrance of running it upside down before, particularly at high-speed.\" A special device, built by Doc Reed, allowed camera operator Jamie Anderson to lie flat with the camera underneath him. Grips carefully maneuvered the ponderous rig so that it would not bang into the small buildings, always making certain that the camera maintained the requisite six-inch shooting height. This sequence - like most of the scenes featuring the dinosaurs - was shot at sixty frames per second to create the illusion of scale and substance. The course of creating first-rate opticals did not always run smoothly for the Anderson/ Meador team. And although some of the final composites or split-screens were not as successful as 66 • CINEFEX 22"],["67","Baby","they had hoped, their efforts represented the best work possible given the existing elements and the ever-limited time factor. Of major concern was the fact that all of the film's opticals had to be double-duped' in order to conform to the final release print - a situation which resulted in an inevitable loss of quality. The optical elements, though shot in VistaVision, had to be reduced through an optical printer to conform to the nonanamorphic Supertechniscope format - which then had to be duped to widescreen anamorphic for final release. \"It was a tough show for us,\" said Meador. \"There were no easy bluescreens in this picture. The high-speed Kodak 5294 film could only be used as a background plate by overexposing it on the printer to minimize the grain. All of our foregrounds were shot on the slower 5247. \"One of our biggest optical problems in integrating the foreground plates with the background was the blue spill from the wet foliage. John Alcott liked to wet down all the trees and plants in the African jungle scenes - which made it look great, but created lots of headaches for us. We didn't use much water on the foliage for the miniatures on stage because it reflected the blue backing and caused a chewy matte line. Even elements like Katt's blond hair and the grey in McGoohan's beard were problems for us.\" During the location photography, John Alcott opted not to use a number 85 filter, resulting in a higher biased blue and a low red content on the negative. The decision served to add richness to the African jungles, but did not match optically with the indoor footage shot at the studio, which was photographed with tungsten lighting. Use of Supertechniscope also complicated the normally routine color timing process. Additional problems surfaced in visually integrating the lighting from foreground to background. Elements shot in the warm dust-filled African skies - a result of lingering sand blown in from the Sahara during the Harmattan season - were tough to match with elements shot in sunny Southern California. \"Optical shows usually love dark moody night sequences,\" explained Anderson, \"because they conceal matte lines better. In Baby, most of the effects take place in broad daylight. During postproduction, we sometimes had to do as many as ten or fourteen recompositings on the optical printer to make the composite good enough for the eye to accept. Optical printer operator Mike Peed deserves credit for seeing it through.\" Despite the tremendous problems suffered by the special photographic process crew, there are several split-screen effects of which they are quite proud. One in particular is the scene in which the mother dinosaur breaks loose from her bindings and begins her wild rampage through Destruction Village. \"The miniature set was built to our specifications,\" said Meador, \"based on a plate that had been shot in Africa. A rotoclip was placed in the camera and we took it from there. Color-wise it was also a good match.\" Another is the final shot in the film, where the mother dinosaur and her hatchling swim off into the sunset. \"The attitude of the animals as they were shot in Africa just didn't make it. The full-size creatures were floating very high, and it didn't look like they were swimming at all. So we reshot the whole scene up at Lake Castaic, using the miniature mother and the real tiny, miniature baby. Baby was on a rail, and there was a diver underneath Mom controlling her movements. We shot it at 48 frames per second, so it looked as if they were really swimming. We faced the cameras into the east sun - which gave us lots of highlights - and kept the water dark so it would match the African location. Matte artist Mike Lloyd then used a VistaVision plate of a sunset and created in the optical printer the split-screen shot that you see in the film.\" Problems and headaches notwithstanding, dinosaur builder Izzy Raponi is basically happy with his work on the film. Under different circumstances, he believes the dinosaurs could have looked consistently credible rather than intermittently, as it ended up. \"If I had to do it over again, I would build Baby in the same basic way - but I wouldn't do it at all unless there was enough time to refine everything and make adjustments. Time is so important, and on Baby there just wasn't enough. The eyes needed to be different. We also wanted to experiment with water bags inside the suit to make the body seem more alive and fluid. But there were just too many changes all along. No one seemed to realize that a change in size meant a major change in everything -including the mechanics. Those kinds of things took away from the time we had to really perfect our dinosaur and get it right.\" \"Baby was a wonderful creature,\" Bill Norton summarized, \"who occasionally was a bit of a bitch. I actually didn't have many expectations about dealing with a cabled dinosaur - because I'd never worked with one before. I was open to seeing the way one worked. We had only one screen test with Baby. Then we went over to Africa and discovered we were doing a few things wrong. We found the correct way to do things and how to cover some things up when they didn't work. We learned as we went - but, unfortunately, we really had very little time for That most basic element of time, or - perhaps more precisely - the lack of it, combined with the demanding factors of a special effects film and an exotic location such as Africa did not easily add up to the equation known as Baby. \"It's tough enough to do an effects movie,\" Jonathan Taplin concluded, \"but it's even tougher when you're someplace like Africa. I would say that the only mistake we made was that we were overly optimistic about our ability to make large-scale special effects work with some kind of ease in a remote location thousands of miles from Burbank. We had this crazed American sense of organization and time, which is nonexistent in the African consciousness. No one in the Ivory Coast had ever made a real movie before. They had no knowledge or sense of what it involved. We were like an Army shut off from supply lines. But it was definitely worth it - we couldn't have made Baby on a Hollywood lot. Africa is the last great frontier for filmmakers and I think it will come to be an increasingly popular place to His escape thwarted by a crash, Kiviat gets his comeuppance in the film's finale when the craneoperated mama grabs a fatal bite. Photographed on the Disney backlot, McGoohan's stunt double is hoisted via wire from a second crane, and then carefully aligned with the mechanical dinosaur's jaws. CINEFEX 22 • 67"],["68","Closing pages",""]]});
